\begin_layout Subsection
Control Theory Formalism
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Control-Theory-Formalism"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Prior to exploring the motivations for learning-based control methods,
 the control theory formalism underlying this thesis must be defined.
 Control theory consists in studying the means of influencing the behavior of a dynamical system to achieve a desired state 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{\text{ref}}$
\end_inset

 or trajectory.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The evolution of a physical system over time is governed by its dynamics.
 In the context of digital control,
 a discrete-time representation is defined such as the next state 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t+1}$
\end_inset

 is a function of the current state 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

 and the applied action 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
s_{t+1}=f\left(s_{t},u_{t}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{U}\mapsto\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

 represents the transition function.
 In classical control theory,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is assumed to be a known,
 stationary first-principles model.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A first-principles model is a mathematical model of a dynamical system derived directly from fundamental physical laws (such as conservation of mass,
 energy,
 momentum or Newton's laws).
 The states and parameters of the model have a clear physical meaning rather than being fitted purely from data 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "johnson2021using"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "First-principles model"
description "A first-principles model is a mathematical model of a dynamical system derived directly from fundamental physical laws (such as conservation of mass, energy, momentum or Newton's laws). The states and parameters of the model have a clear physical meaning rather than being fitted purely from data \\cite{johnson2021using}. "
literal "true"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This interaction between the system and the controller is traditionally represented as a closed-loop system as illustrated in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Control-Loop"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 The controller can perceive the states of the system and act on it through actuators.
 The sets representing the possible states (respectively actions) are referred to as the state space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

,
 (respectively action space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{U}$
\end_inset

).
 The state space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

 represents all possible configurations of the system (e.g joint angles,
 velocities).
 The state of the system at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is denoted as 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}\in\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

.
 The action space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{U}$
\end_inset

 is the set of all possible control inputs (e.g.,
 motor torques,
 voltage).
 The action at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 applied on the system is denoted as 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}\in\mathcal{U}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement document
alignment document
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename assets/Control_Loop.pdf
	width 70col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Illustration of Closed-Loop Control.
 The 
\emph on
System
\emph default
 block corresponds to the targeted dynamical system to be controlled.
 The transition dynamics are described by function 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

.
 The state 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

 is transmitted to the 
\emph on
Controller
\emph default
,
 whose behavioral policy is described by function 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
 from the 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

 the controller choses an action 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 to be applied on the 
\emph on
System
\emph default
.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:Control-Loop"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The controller follows a goal that is mathematically described by the minimization of a cost function.
 For a given horizon 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 (that could be equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $\infty$
\end_inset

),
 the objective is to minimize the cumulative cost 
\begin_inset Formula $J$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
J\left(s_{t:t+T},u_{t:t+T-1}\right)=\sum_{k=0}^{T-1}l\left(s_{t+k},u_{t+k}\right)+l_{T}\left(s_{t+T}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t:t+T}\in\mathcal{S}^{T}$
\end_inset

 is a state trajectory,
 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t:t+T-1}\in\mathcal{U}^{T-1}$
\end_inset

 is a sequence of actions,
 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 is the stage cost (e.g.
 tracking error or energy consumption) and 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{T}$
\end_inset

 is the terminal cost.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Depending on the task to be accomplished and its requirements,
 systems can be subject to physical and safety limits.
 These are expressed as algebraic inequalities such as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
g\left(s_{t},u_{t}\right)\leq0\,\,\text{or}\,\,h\left(s_{t},u_{t}\right)=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

These constraints may represent hard limits (e.g.
 maximum torque) or safety boundaries (e.g.
 speed limits,
 minimum distance to human operator).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 the control law 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

 encapsulates the decision-making logic such as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
u_{t}=\pi\left(s_{t}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The cost function,
 the constraints and the transition function are part of the standard Optimal Control Problem (OCP),
 which serves as the template for the controllers discussed in the remainder of this chapter.
 An OCP is formulated as following:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\min_{u_{t:t+T-1}}J\left(s_{t:t+T},u_{t:t+T-1}\right)=\min_{u_{t:t+T-1}}\,\,\, & \sum_{k=0}^{T-1}l\left(s_{t+k},u_{t+k}\right)+l_{T}\left(s_{t+T}\right)\label{eq:OCP}\\
\text{s.t.\,\,\, } & s_{t+k+1}=f\left(s_{t+k},u_{t+k}\right)\nonumber \\
 & u_{t+k}\in\mathcal{U},\,\,s_{t+k}\in\mathcal{S},\,\,k\in\left\{ 0,\dots,T\right\} \nonumber \\
 & g\left(s_{t+k},u_{t+k}\right)\leq0\nonumber \\
 & h\left(s_{t+k},u_{t+k}\right)=0\nonumber 
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The existence and quality of a solution to the OCP defined above depends on the following three properties of the system 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ogata2020modern"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Controllability:
 
\series default
This property assesses whether the control input 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 can drive the system from any initial state 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{0}$
\end_inset

 to any desired state 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

 within a finite time.
 This property is not always guaranteed,
 as real-world systems often have fewer actuators than state variables.
 For example,
 a car has a steering wheel and an accelerator,
 but it cannot move sideways directly.
 Its lateral position cannot be changed independently of its forward motion.
 In this example,
 the system is 
\series bold
globally controllable
\series default
 (the car can go anywhere) but 
\series bold
locally constrained
\series default
 (the sideways movement needs more steps).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Observability:
 
\series default
This corresponds to the ability to 
\series bold
reconstruct the state
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

 solely from available measurements.
 It is not always guaranteed,
 as sensors may be noisy or missing entirely.
 For example,
 an eco-driving device might lack a sensor for the road slope.
 If the device cannot infer the slope by observing changes in speed or engine load,
 the state is partially observable and the efficiency might be compromised.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Stabilizability:
 
\series default
Even if a system is not fully controllable,
 it is considered stabilizable if all the unstable modes can be brought to a steady state using the available actions.
 This property focuses on whether the controller can prevent the system from blowing-up or crashing.
 For example,
 a self-balancing vehicle (e.g.
 a Segway) is inherently unstable as the center of mass resides above the pivot point.
 While the actuators are only able to apply horizontal force on the base (making it not fully controllable),
 the system remains stabilizable because those horizontal movements can be modulated to maintain the vertical equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The existence and quality of a solution to the OCP defined above depends on the following three properties of the system:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Controllability:
 
\series default
This property assesses whether the control input 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 can drive the system from any initial state 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{0}$
\end_inset

 to any desired state 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

 within a finite time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Observability:
 
\series default
This corresponds to the ability to reconstruct the state 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

 from available measurements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Stabilizability:
 
\series default
Even if a system is not fully controllable,
 it is considered stabilizable if all the unstable modes can be brought to a steady state using the available actions.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Beyond the internal properties of the system,
 the effectiveness of a controller is governed by the complexity of the considered system.
 This is formalized by Ashby's Law of Requisite Variety 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ashby1956introduction"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 which states that for a controller to effectively manage a system,
 its internal variety,
 i.e the number of possible states,
 must be at least equal to the variety of the disturbances it aims to compensate for.
 In other words,
 a controller must be 
\series bold
at least as complex
\series default
 as the task of keeping the goal satisfied.
 An extension of this theorem,
 the Conant-Ashby theorem 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "conant1970every"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 states that every good controller of a system 
\series bold
must be a model of that system
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To ground this formalism in an physical example that satisfies these foundational properties,
 let's consider the classical pendulum problem 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "brockman2016openai,towers2024gymnasium"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 A mass 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 is attached to a pivot by a rod of length 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

.
 The objective is to control its angular position 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 using a motor that applies a torque 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 at the pivot (c.f Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Illustration-of-the-Pendulum"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 The components of the problem are the following:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
State
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

:
 A state is represented by the angle and angular velocity at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 such as 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}=\left[\theta,\dot{\theta}\right]^{\top}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Action 
\series default

\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

:
 An action corresponds to the application of a torque on the joint of the pendulum.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Dynamics 
\series default

\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

:
 The dynamics are derived from Newton's Second Law,
 where the next state depends on gravity,
 damping and the applied torque 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Constraints
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(g,h\right)$
\end_inset

:
 An inequality constraint 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 represents the physical limit of the torque that can be applied by the motor (
\begin_inset Formula $g\left(s_{t},u_{t}\right)=\left|u\right|-u_{\max}\leq0$
\end_inset

) while an equality constraint 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 enforces the dynamics (
\begin_inset Formula $h\left(s_{t},u_{t}\right)=f\left(s_{t},u_{t}\right)-s_{t+1}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Objective 
\series default

\begin_inset Formula $J$
\end_inset

:
 The cost function penalizes the distance from a target angle (e.g reaching 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=0$
\end_inset

) while minimizing the energy 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}^{2}$
\end_inset

 consumed by the motor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The pendulum is a fully controllable,
 observable and stabilizable system.
 Since the transition function 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is perfectly known and the system possesses these mathematical properties,
 it is a 
\series bold
\emph on
perfect
\emph default
 control problem
\series default
.
 For this reason,
 it represents a simple baseline system on which control algorithms can be tested before they are applied to more complex high-dimensional systems.
\end_layout

