\begin_layout Subsection
From SACL to CELL
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:From-SACL-to-Coupled-Learning-and-Control"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The inspiration for the CELL formalism lies in the Self-Adaptive Context Learning (SACL) paradigm 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "boesSelfAdaptiveContextLearning2015"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 SACL was originally proposed to solve complex control problems by learning to map perceived states,
 referred to as contexts,
 to the most effective actions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
The SACL Paradigm
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In SACL,
 the controller is defined through two primary entities:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
The Adaptation Mechanism
\series default
:
 Its goal is to build,
 maintain and provide reliable knowledge about the current context and potential actions.
 It correlates the activity of the controller to perceived states of the environment to identify contextual boundaries and model local dynamics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
The Exploitation Mechanism
\series default
:
 This entity is responsible for applying actions on the dynamical system.
 It uses the knowledge provided by the Adaptation Mechanism,
 alongside domain-specific constraints to decide which control action to apply.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The interactions between these two layers is governed by a continuous information loop and described in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:SACL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 The Adaptation Mechanism perceives the environment and proposes possible actions,
 and the Exploitation Mechanism then chooses an action to perform.
 The latter then emits a feedback that is sent to the Adaptation Mechanism to refine the context-action mapping.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement document
alignment document
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename assets/SACL.pdf
	lyxscale 50
	width 70col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The Self-Adaptive Context Learning (SACL) Paradigm
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:SACL"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Adaptation Mechanism relies on a set of self-organizing 
\emph on
Context
\emph default
 agents.
 These agents are the core adaptive software entities that implement the social learning component of the system.
 Each 
\emph on
Context
\emph default
 agent is defined by a tripartite structure representing a specific tile of knowledge:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Context
\series default
:
 This is a set of validity ranges,
 one for each input feature.
 An agent is considered valid only when the perceived state lies within all corresponding ranges.
 These ranges represent a tile in the input space that allow mapping the current perceived state to a specific action and its effects on the system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Action
\series default
:
 It corresponds to a control command to be applied to the dynamical system (e.g.
 torque setpoint or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

go forward
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 command).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Appreciation
\series default
:
 It corresponds to a forecast of the action's effect on the controlled system.
 It is often expressed as a confidence or utility value.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Based on the feedback provided by the Exploitation Mechanism,
 the valid agents that proposed actions can assess the quality of their proposition,
 allowing them to improve.
 The SACL architecture enables dynamic cooperative self-organization through the detection and management of Non-Cooperative Situations (NCS).
 NCS are situations of functional inadequacy regarding agent cooperation,
 that may occur during the self-organization process when agents deviate from their nominal cooperative behavior due to perception failures or erroneous decisions.
 The adaptation mechanisms defined to resolve or anticipate these NCS constitute the learning rules governing the collective behavior of 
\emph on
Context
\emph default
 agents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Despite several successful applications 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "boes2017self,verstaevel2017lifelong"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 SACL presents two primary limitations:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Myopic Behavior
\series default
:
 SACL is mainly designed to operate from instantaneous feedback.
 This results in a myopic controller because agents are rewarded based on the immediate success of the proposed action.
 Thus,
 the controller struggles to anticipate consequences beyond a single time step.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Domain-Specific Knowledge
\series default
:
 SACL heavily relies on domain-specific knowledge to design the feedback functions.
 These functions are used to determine if an agent's Appreciation is erroneous or inexact.
 This limits the generalizability of the approach.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
From Behavioral Control to the CELL Formalism
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Building on prior works that leveraged SACL principles to design machine learning algorithms rather than control strategies 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "nigonAPPRENTISSAGEARTIFICIELADAPTE2017,fourez2024analyzing"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 we introduce the CELL formalism.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
CELL is a formalism that provides a framework for designing online learning algorithms based on a self-organizing ensemble of local expert agents governed by social rules.
 Rather than acting as a standalone behavioral controller,
 CELL focuses on building a predictive model of a dynamical system,
 intended for integration within a MPC architecture.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The transition from SACL to CELL can be summarized into three shifts in how the multi-agent system is used:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
From Action Suggestion to Function Approximation
\series default
:
 In SACL,
 the objective of the multi-agent system is to suggest actions (
\emph on
control
\emph default
) and evaluate a utility-based appreciation.
 In CELL,
 the objective of the multi-agent system is to predict the future states of the system (
\emph on
machine learning
\emph default
).
 The goal is to provide a faithful local approximation of the transition function 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{U}\mapsto\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

,
 such that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
s_{t+1}=f\left(s_{t},u_{t}\right)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t+1}\in\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

 is the next state,
 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}\in\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

 is the current state,
 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}\in\mathcal{U}$
\end_inset

 the applied action.
 By focusing solely on the machine learning task,
 CELL eliminates the need to design domain-specific feedback functions for each individual control problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
From Myopia to Anticipation
\series default
:
 The self-organization of agents in SACL is primarily driven by instantaneous feedback,
 which limits the controller to a reactive,
 one-step-ahead horizon.
 In contrast,
 CELL operates as a predictive model of the system's future dynamics.
 When integrated within a MPC framework,
 it enables the controller to simulate and evaluate multiple future trajectories over a receding horizon.
 This makes CELL capable of anticipating longer-term outcomes compared to SACL myopic behavior.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
From External Advice to Tight Coupling
\series default
:
 While SACL treats the multi-agent system as an external advisor providing action proposals,
 CELL is tightly coupled with the control layer within MPC,
 as shown in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:CELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 The MPC optimizer does not wait for agent propositions,
 instead,
 it actively queries the ensemble of local expert agents at high frequency to predict future states or to compute gradients necessary for trajectory optimization.
 This architecture combines the adaptive,
 self-organizing strengths of multi-agent systems with the mathematical robustness of optimization-based control.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following sections detail the CELL formalism,
 beginning with the definition of its core component:
 the Local Expert Agents.
\end_layout

