\begin_layout Subsection
Local Expert Agents
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Local-Expert-Agents"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The previous section established the relationship between CELL and the SACL paradigm,
 highlighting the transition from a behavioral control framework to a predictive dynamics model integrated within a MPC architecture.
 This section defines the notion of Local Expert Agent within CELL.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 acts as a local expert for a specific area within the input space.
 An agent is defined by a triplet such as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{A}_{i}=\left\{ \phi_{i},f_{i},\mathcal{M}_{i}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\mapsto\left[0,1\right]$
\end_inset

 is an activation function,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\mapsto\mathbb{R}^{m}$
\end_inset

 is an internal local model and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents the internal state of the agent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\series bold
activation function
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

 defines the spatial boundaries in which the agent is considered an expert (i.e.
 relevant for making predictions).
 For any given input vector 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}\left(x\right)\in\left[0,1\right]$
\end_inset

 returns an activation value.
 This value is used by a mechanism to select the most relevant agents to predict for 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 This mechanism is called a spatial gating mechanism and it will be fully detailed in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Operational-Pipelines"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 An activation value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}\left(x\right)=1$
\end_inset

 implies that the agent is an expert to predict for 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 It means that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is In-Distribution (ID) for agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Conversely,
 an activation value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}\left(x\right)=0$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is outside the area of expertise of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 It means that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is Out-of-Distribution (OoD).
 The geometry of this function can be anything and can be adapted to the problem encountered and to specific requirements related to the dimensionality of inputs or computation time (e.g.
 computing RBF kernels values in 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is more computationally heavy than computing intersection between 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and a set of rectangles).
 An example of rectangular activation function is given in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Rectangle-Activation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 Ultimately,
 the activation function answers to the following question:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center

\emph on
When should 
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
predict ?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement document
alignment document
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename assets/Example_Orthotope_Activation_Function.pdf
	lyxscale 50
	width 70col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Representation of the areas of expertise of 2 local expert agents (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{1}$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{2}$
\end_inset

) where the activation functions 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

 correspond to a rectangle.
 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{2}$
\end_inset

 represents the dimensions of the input space .
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:Rectangle-Activation"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The local 
\series bold
internal model
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

 contains the predictive expertise of the agent,
 which is relevant locally within the area of expertise defined by the activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Each agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 maintains its own set of parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{i}$
\end_inset

 to map input values 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 to predictions values such as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{y_{i}}=f_{i}\left(x,\theta_{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y_{i}}$
\end_inset

 is the prediction of agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 for input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 This formalism is model-agnostic,
 meaning that virtually any class of learning model could be used as internal model for the agents.
 Ultimately,
 the internal model answers to the following question:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center

\emph on
What should 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 predict?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\series bold
internal state
\series default
 (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

) encapsulates metadata about the agent (e.g.
 local performance metrics,
 maturity,
 age,
 ...) and may also include a memory of the most recently encountered training points (in the form of 
\begin_inset Formula $x,y$
\end_inset

 feature-target tuples).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By separating the spatialization layer (activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

) from the modeling layer (internal model 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

),
 each component can be independently optimized or replaced.
 It allows CELL to remain agnostic to both the choice of activation function and the class of internal model employed within local expert agents.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The global prediction of the model is a result of the collective output of the local expert agent that compose it.
 The state of convergence and learned model representation of the system can be directly gathered by inspecting the internal states 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and the spatial distribution of agents across the input space through the parameters of the activation functions 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In addition to these fundamental components,
 agents are associated with actions that allow them to adapt individually and locally.
 Two types of actions must be defined in the context of CELL to enable local self-organization:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Shape Update
\series default
:
 This action corresponds to updating the parameters of the activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 which effectively changes the shape of the area of expertise of agent
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Depending on the feedback received for its prediction on an input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 the agent may realize either that his area of expertise is either well positioned within the input space or not.
 For example,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 receives a bad feedback for its prediction on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 it might shrink its area of expertise to not include 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 anymore.
 Conversely,
 if it receives a good feedback,
 it might expand its area of expertise to include 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Model Update
\series default
:
 It corresponds to updating the parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{i}$
\end_inset

 of the internal model 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

 to include a point 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 For example,
 it could consist in a gradient descent step.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The next section describes the inference and learning pipelines responsible for agent self-organization and global model prediction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Operational Pipelines:
 Inference and Learning
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Operational-Pipelines"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After having formalized the main components that compose an agent in previous section,
 this section presents 
\series bold
the training loop of a CELL system
\series default
 and how 
\series bold
the global predictions
\series default
 are obtained from the set of local expert agents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A CELL system operates through 2 pipelines:
 the 
\series bold
inference pipeline
\series default
,
 which produces a global prediction from the ensemble of agents,
 and the 
\series bold
learning pipeline
\series default
,
 which governs the social self-organization of agents.
 Both pipelines rely on a 
\series bold
spatial gating mechanism
\series default
 to manage the locality in the system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a given input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 the spatial gating mechanism selects a subset of local expert agents from the ensemble that are the most relevant for prediction.
 This spatial gating mechanism guarantees 
\series bold
spatial isolation of parameters
\series default
 inside the learning system.
 For any input,
 the spatial gating mechanism outputs two subsets of agents:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Activated Agents
\series default
 (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{activated}}$
\end_inset

):
 Agents for which 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 falls within their area of expertise (i.e 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is ID for activated agents and OoD for non activated agents).
 These agents are the most relevant agents to contribute to the final prediction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Neighbor Agents
\series default
 (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

):
 The set of neighbor agents is a superset of activated agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{activated}}$
\end_inset

.
 In addition to including activated agents,
 it includes agents for which 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is not yet ID but is close to their spatial boundaries.
 The notion of closeness can be defined as a distance threshold or a minimum activation value 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}<\epsilon$
\end_inset

.
 These agents are candidate for updating the shape of their area of expertise to include 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and become expert on it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To obtain the global prediction of the ensemble of local expert agents,
 the 
\series bold
inference pipeline
\series default
 aims to select the most relevant subset of agents from an input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 and to aggregate their local predictions.
 The inference process,
 illustrated in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:CELL-Inference-Pipeline"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 follows four distinct steps:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Distance Evaluation
\series default
:
 The degree of expertise on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 of each individual agent is computed using their activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Spatial Gating
\series default
 
\series bold
(Expert Selection)
\series default
:
 The spatial gating mechanism filters the ensemble of selected agents to identify the most relevant agents to predict for 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 and outputs two sets of agents:
 the activated agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{activated}}$
\end_inset

 and the neighbor agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

.
 For example,
 the activated and neighbor agents sets can be filtered based on a threshold on the activation value such as 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}\left(x\right)\geq\tau_{\text{activation}}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}\left(x\right)\geq\tau_{\text{neighbor}}$
\end_inset

(where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{\text{activation}}\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{\text{neighbor}}\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 are two real scalar thresholds).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Local Prediction
\series default
:
 Each selected agent (neighbor or activated depending on the prediction policy defined by the designer of the system) produces a prediction proposal based on its internal model:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{i}=f_{i}\left(x,\theta_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Aggregation
\series default
:
 The individual prediction of the most relevant experts are combined using an aggregation function (e.g.
 weighted average).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
