\begin_layout Standard
The 
\series bold
learning pipeline
\series default
 governs the evolution of the population of agents through social rules.
 Unlike traditional gradient descent that updates all parameters globally,
 the spatial gating mechanism in CELL is leveraged to ensure parameter isolation by exposing only relevant agents to new data.
 For a given feature-target tuple 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(x,y\right)$
\end_inset

,
 a typical learning step follows five stages as illustrated in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:CELL-Training-Pipeline"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Distance Evaluation
\series default
:
 The activation value 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 of each agent in the ensemble is evaluated to determine its expertise on input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Expert Selection (Gating)
\series default
:
 The spatial gating mechanism identifies the sets of activated agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{activated}}$
\end_inset

 and neighbor agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

.
 This spatial selection ensures that only agents with relevant expertise (or potential for expertise) are candidates for being updated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Local Prediction
\series default
:
 Each selected agent computes its local prediction 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{i}=f_{i}\left(x,\theta_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Social Feedback Computation
\series default
:
 Each selected agent receives a feedback that determines its subsequent updates.
 This signal can encapsulate two components:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Absolute Feedback
\series default
:
 Based on the individual local error,
 it assesses if the agent's internal model is currently a faithful representation of the local dynamics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Relative/Social Feedback
\series default
:
 The agent's performance is compared to that of other close agents or to a global baseline (e.g.
 a running linear regression).
 This allows social dynamics such as 
\emph on
competition
\emph default
 (where only the best expert is allowed to stay activated for a region) or 
\emph on
cooperation
\emph default
 (where multiple agents refine their boundaries to collectively cover a complex transition).
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Application of Learning Rules
\series default
:
 Based on the feedback received and on the agent's internal state 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 specific social rules are triggered.
 These rules translates the social feedback into concrete structural or parametric changes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\series bold
learning rules
\series default
 within CELL are the primary drivers of self-organization.
 Each is applied only to a specific set of agents,
 typically the activated agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{activated}}$
\end_inset

,
 the neighbors agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

 or the k-closest agents.
 They are triggered by conditions related to the feedback values or the agent's internal memory (e.g.
 maturity or historical performance).
 When,
 triggered,
 a learning rule performs an action.
 These actions can be categorized into two types:
 
\emph on
Individual Actions
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
Meta-Actions
\emph default
.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the one hand,
 
\emph on
Individual Actions
\emph default
 are targeted specifically at an agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 to modify an element of its tripartite structure.
 As specified in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Local-Expert-Agents"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 we remind that it includes the 
\series bold
Model Update
\series default
 which adjusts the internal parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 and the 
\series bold
Shape Update
\series default
 which modifies the parameters of the activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

 to expand or shrink the area of expertise.
 On the other hand,
 
\emph on
Meta-Actions
\emph default
 are actions that manage the population and affect the composition of the ensemble of agents.
 
\series bold
Agent Creation
\series default
 allows the system to remain non-parametric by spawning new experts in under-represented regions of the input space,
 while 
\series bold
Agent Destruction
\series default
 ensures parsimony and prevents uncontrolled population growth by removing redundant or consistently inaccurate agents.
\end_layout

