\begin_layout Subsection
Discussion on Theoretical Properties
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Discussion-on-Theoretical-Properties"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After having established established the relationship between SACL and the CELL formalism,
 the previous sections formalized its components and detailed the operational pipelines that govern the ensemble of local expert agents.
 This section discusses the theoretical properties of CELL systems,
 highlighting how it addresses the challenges of online learning in non-stationary environments and its suitability for integration within a control framework.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A primary challenge in online learning for dynamical system is 
\series bold
catastrophic forgetting
\series default
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kirkpatrick2017overcoming"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 where new information overwrites previously learned knowledge.
 CELL inherently mitigates this through its spatial gating mechanism,
 as it ensures 
\series bold
spatial isolation of parameters
\series default
.
 Since learning rules and parameter updates are restricted to the sets 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{activated}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

,
 data from one region of the input space cannot easily corrupt the expertise of agents in non-related regions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While traditional machine learning often relies on top-down global optimization,
 CELL is inherently multi-agent and adopts a bottom-up design based on social learning.
 In the context of CELL,
 the global dynamics model is not a predefined static structure but an 
\series bold
emergent property
\series default
 of the self-organizing ensemble of local experts.
 Each agent operates with only partial knowledge of the global problem,
 
\series bold
perceives
\series default
 its local social context,
 
\series bold
decides
\series default
 on a rule to trigger based on its own feedback,
 and 
\series bold
acts
\series default
 to update its internal components.
 This decentralized decision-making process brings structural adaptability.
 It allows the model to modify its own internal structure through agent creation or destruction without the computational burden or re-optimizing a global topology.
 By shifting the complexity from the model's architecture to the social dynamics between agents,
 the system can autonomously 
\series bold
expand its capacity in response to non-stationary dynamics
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Furthermore,
 the formalism is 
\series bold
model-agnostic
\series default
,
 meaning that virtually any class of learning model could be used as the internal prediction model of an agent.
 This modularity favors a high level of transparency in the inner workings of the learning system.
 In the remainder of this thesis,
 we focus on simple,
 low-complexity models that can converge quickly,
 such as 
\series bold
linear regression
\series default
.
 Indeed,
 high-capacity models could be counterproductive because they would not receive enough local data to converge,
 thereby hurting global performance.
 Using fast-converging model classes ensures better online learning performance and maintains the white-box nature of the inner workings,
 which represents a significant advantage over global black box models for safety-critical applications.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 the integration of CELL within an optimization-based control framework like MPC necessitates some properties like 
\series bold
differentiability and fast inference
\series default
 (both of which are discussed in detail in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Solving-Control-Tasks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 While the agents provide local expertise,
 the aggregation function should ensure the global prediction handles smoothly the transitions between the areas of expertise of agents.
 If the internal models 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 the activation functions 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

 and the aggregation function are all differentiable,
 the ensemble of local expert agents produces differentiable predictions.
 This allows for the extraction of the necessary gradients 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla_{x}f$
\end_inset

 (i.e the Jacobians of the dynamics with respect to state and action) for optimizing control trajectories within an MPC framework with a gradient-based solver.
 This property will be discussed more in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:MPC-and-Local-Linearization"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 of Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Solving-Control-Tasks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The real-time constraints of control loops require the model to perform high-frequency inference.
 In CELL instantiations,
 fast inference is facilitated both by the use of low-complexity internal models and by the spatial gating mechanism that selects only a subset of relevant agents for prediction.
 However,
 maintaining a fast inference speed becomes challenging as the population grows.
 Indeed,
 evaluating the activation value 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 of a very large number of agents can rapidly represent a 
\series bold
computational bottleneck
\series default
.
 Consequently,
 achieving efficient scaling requires a carefully designed spatialization layer to minimize the cost of distance evaluations and ensure that the spatial gating mechanism remains compatible with the timing requirements of the control task to solve.
 This topic will be discussed in detail in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Efficient-Implementation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 of Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Solving-Control-Tasks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To transition from the theoretical design framework to practical applications,
 the following sections 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:oCELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:kCELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 introduce two distinct instantiations of CELL:
 oCELL and kCELL.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
oCELL:
 CELL with Orthotope Spatialization
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:oCELL"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Previously we described the CELL formalism which establishes the operational logic of the ensemble of expert agents while remaining agnostic to the activation function,
 to the internal model classes and to the social feedback used.
 Consequently,
 to transform this theoretical framework into a functional algorithm,
 one must defined the concrete form of the activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

 and the specific set of social rules governing the population.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first instantiation of CELL,
 oCELL,
 orthotope-based CELL,
 leverages axis-aligned orthotopes to define agent boundaries.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
An 
\series bold
orthotope
\series default
 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

-dimensional generalization of a rectangle 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "johnson2018geometries"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "Orthotope"
description "An orthotope is the n-dimensional generalization of a rectangle \\cite{johnson2018geometries}."
literal "true"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This choice builds on previous works on this family of multi-agent systems 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "dato2021apprentissage,fourez2024analyzing"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 It prioritizes computational efficiency and geometric interpretability providing a baseline for evaluating the emergent modeling capabilities of the CELL framework in low dimensional input spaces.
 oCELL serves as a primary mean for demonstrating how emergent introspective properties of this ensemble of local expert agents provide useful insights about the learning process.
 This introspective properties are shown to be related to the traditional notions of explainability and interpretability in machine learning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This section is structured as follows.
 First,
 Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Local-Expert-Agents-oCELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 defines the tripartite structure of a local expert agent in oCELL,
 detailing how orthotopic boundaries and local models are mathematically formulated.
 Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Learning-Rules-oCELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 then presents the behavioral blueprint of the system through its specific learning rules and the resulting rule table.
 To validate the effectiveness of this approach,
 Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Comparative-Study"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 provides a comparative analysis of oCELL against state-of-the-art ensemble learning algorithms on a two-dimensional regression benchmark.
 Afterwards,
 Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Introspective-Properties-oCELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 introduces a set of metrics derived from the spatial distribution of agents,
 demonstrating how the structure of local expert agents can be exploited to monitor the learning and characterize the underlying data distribution.
 Finally,
 Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Limitations"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 presents a critical analysis of oCELL,
 its properties and limitations that subsequently lead to the development of kCELL in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:kCELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Local Expert Agents
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Local-Expert-Agents-oCELL"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this section,
 the tripartite structure of oCELL's local expert agents is described in detail.
 Each agent 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{A}_{i}=\left\{ \phi_{i},f_{i},\mathcal{M}_{i}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

 is a specialized local expert that occupies an orthotope-shaped area in the input space,
 maintains a local linear model and manages a private memory of its past experiences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

 defines the area of expertise in the input space in which an agent considers itself as an expert.
 In oCELL,
 for a 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

-dimensional input space,
 the area of expertise of agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is defined by an orthotope 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{i}$
\end_inset

 parameterized by a set of lower bounds 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{i,j}$
\end_inset

 and upper bounds 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{i,j}$
\end_inset

 for each dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $j\in\left\{ 1,\dots,d\right\} $
\end_inset

 such as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{i}=\left[l_{i,1},h_{i,1}\right]\times\dots\times\left[l_{i,d},h_{i,d}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The hard boundaries defined by the orthotopes allow for the use of geometric operations such as volume measurement or intersection testing.
 The volume 
\begin_inset Formula $V\left(\mathcal{H}_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

 of an orthotope is defined as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V\left(\mathcal{H}_{i}\right)=\prod_{j=1}^{d}\left(h_{i,j}-l_{i,j}\right)\label{eq:orthotope-volume-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

An input 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$
\end_inset

 is considered as intersecting an orthotope 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{i}$
\end_inset

 if,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\forall j\in\left\{ 1,\dots,d\right\} ,\,\,l_{i,j}\leq x_{i,j}\leq h_{i,j}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Furthermore,
 an orthotope 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{1}$
\end_inset

 intersects another orthotope 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{2}$
\end_inset

 if,
 for all dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\max\left(l_{1,j},l_{2,j}\right)\leq\min\left(h_{1,j},h_{2,j}\right)\label{eq:orthotope-intersection-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{1,j}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $l_{2,j}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{1,j}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $h_{2,j}$
\end_inset

 denote the lower and upper bounds of respectively 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Agents in oCELL are constructed based on the assumption of 
\emph on
uniform knowledge
\emph default
:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{assumption}[Uniform Knowledge]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

The level of expertise of an agent is assumed to be uniform over its area of expertise for any point intersecting it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{assumption}
\end_layout

\end_inset

Therefore,
 the 
\series bold
activation function
\series default
 of an agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a binary function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\mapsto\left\{ 0,1\right\} $
\end_inset

 defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\phi_{i}\left(x\right)=\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if}\,\,x\,\,\text{intersects}\,\,\mathcal{H}_{i}\\
0 & \text{else}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

An input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is said to be In-Distribution (ID) for agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}\left(x\right)=1$
\end_inset

.
 This hard boundary ensures the agent's area of expertise is well localized and contiguous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As the CELL formalism is model-agnostic,
 oCELL is designed to work with 
\series bold
low-complexity models
\series default
 to ensure fast updates and convergence of local models in online settings.
 Each agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 maintains its own local model 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}\left(x,\theta_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{i}$
\end_inset

 denotes the local parameters.
 In the context of this thesis,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

 is typically a local linear regressor:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{y_{i}}=f_{i}\left(x\right)=\Theta_{i}\left[\begin{array}{c}
x\\
1
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times(d+1)}$
\end_inset

 is the parameter matrix representing the local affine transformation of agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}$
\end_inset

 denotes the estimated local prediction.
 With this linear structure,
 each agent provides a first-order approximation of the dynamics within its area of expertise.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The internal state 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

 tracks the local history of observations.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

 includes a local memory buffer 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}_{i}$
\end_inset

 that stores the last observed samples (
\begin_inset Formula $x,y$
\end_inset

).
 This buffer is used to update the internal model 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

 and has a fixed size to allow forgetting through FIFO (First-In-First-Out) updates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To transform the population of experts into a global model,
 oCELL uses a 
\series bold
spatial gating mechanism
\series default
 to select relevant agents.
 For any input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 the spatial gating mechanism selects two sets of local expert agents (among all the agents in the ensemble):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Activated Agents
\series default
 (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{activated}}$
\end_inset

) which contains agents for which 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}\left(x\right)=1$
\end_inset

 (cf.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:oCELL-Activated"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Neighbor Agents
\series default
 (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

) which contains agents whose orthotope 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{i}$
\end_inset

 intersects a neighborhood orthotope 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{\text{neighborhood}}$
\end_inset

 centered on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 The size of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{\text{neighborhood}}$
\end_inset

 is a hyperparameter of the system.
 Tuning this parameter allows the designer to adjust the degree of smoothing in the global prediction.
 (cf.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:oCELL-Neighbors"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The global prediction 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}$
\end_inset

 is obtained by an arithmetic mean of the activated agents' proposals
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{y}=\frac{1}{\left|\mathcal{D}_{\text{activated}}\right|}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{activated}}}\hat{y}_{i}
\]

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{activated}}$
\end_inset

 is empty,
 the ensemble 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

is selected,
 and if 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

 is also empty,
 a fallback mechanism selects the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-closest agents to ensure continuity in the global approximated function.
 This fallback mechanism prevents prediction failures in regions where the system has limited knowledge (i.e poor agent paving of the area around 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

