\begin_layout Subsection
Local Expert Agents
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:kCELL-Context-Agents"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This section describes the tripartite structure of kCELL's local expert agents.
 Each agent is defined as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{A}_{i}=\left\{ \phi_{i},f_{i},\mathcal{M}_{i}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

 denotes its activation function,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

 its internal model,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

 its internal state.
 The agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a specialized local expert that represents its area of expertise through a smooth RBF kernel,
 maintains a local linear model,
 and manages an internal state to track its performance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike oCELL which uses a binary activation function,
 the area of expertise of local expert agents in kCELL are spatialized differently as illustrated in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Comparison-Activation-Surfaces"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 A RBF kernel is used as a continuous and smooth activation function to represent the area of expertise in feature space.
 Compared to using orthotope with hard boundaries,
 this kernel-based spatialization unlocks more degrees of freedom and makes the learned global function smooth and differentiable.
 Thus,
 it makes it more optimization-friendly for use control tasks.
\end_layout

