\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Learning rules of kCELL
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "tab:Learning-rules-of-kCELL"

\end_inset

.
 Each line corresponds to a given learning rule defined by:
 1) a social context representing a condition on the number of local experts 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

 around a new input 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

;
 2) a condition on one of the two types of feedback 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}$
\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 or on the running confidence of agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{C}_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}$
\end_inset

;
 3) a target subset of agents that will be affected by the rule (e.g Closest the subset of agents containing only the agent which is closest to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

);
 and 4) the actions or meta-actions to perform on the agents of the targeted subset.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

To visualize the result of a learning and local cooperation of agents,
 a 1-dimensional example can be used.
 A small synthetic dataset is generated from the function 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(x\right)=\sin\left(x\right)+\epsilon\,\mathcal{N}\left(0,1\right)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 the noise scaling factor.
 Then,
 online learning is simulated by sequentially feeding the feature-target tuples (
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new},y_{new}$
\end_inset

) one at a time to kCELL.
 Each point is only seen once during learning.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:1D-Visualization-of-local-coop"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 presents a visualization of the spatial organization of agents 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "fig:1D-Spatial-organization"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and the resulting predictions 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "fig:1D-Resulting-prediction"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 We can observe that the agents overlap and do not fit perfectly across their entire area of expertise.
 At the edges of agents the error is higher than in the center.
 This is the expected behavior resulting from the assumption of non-uniformity in the knowledge within areas of expertise.
 We observe that the overall predictions approximate closely the function considering the noise added to the data.
 It demonstrates kCELL's ability to generalize effectively by interpolation,
 and it highlights the usefulness of a soft-weighted aggregation function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 from an implementation perspective,
 the learning behavior in kCELL is governed by the following hyperparameters:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\xi\in\left[0,100\right]$
\end_inset

:
 The chi-squared percentile defining a threshold for the gating mechanism to extract the set of neighbor agents from the global population.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $k_{\text{steep}}>0$
\end_inset

:
 The steepness of the tanh function for social feedback normalization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\in\left]0,1\right]$
\end_inset

:
 The smoothing factor for the running exponential moving average of confidence value of agents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{\text{confidence}}\in\left[-1,1\right]$
\end_inset

:
 The survival threshold for agent destruction.
 If an agent's confidence goes below this value,
 it is destroyed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{short}}\in\mathbb{N}^{+}$
\end_inset

:
 The size of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}_{\text{short}}$
\end_inset

 used to fit the linear baseline predictor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{ini}}\in\mathbb{N}^{+}$
\end_inset

:
 The minimal number of points inside 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}_{\text{ini}}$
\end_inset

 needed to create a new agent.
 With linear regressions,
 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{ini}}$
\end_inset

 should ideally be greater than the number of input dimensions such as 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{ini}}>d+1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $k_{\text{social}}>0$
\end_inset

:
 The number of closest agents used to compute the social baseline 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{base}$
\end_inset

 when an agent has no neighbors (
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{neighbors}}=1$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

