key "kingma2017adammethodstochasticoptimization"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Adaptive Hoeffding Trees
\series default
:
 An online decision tree variant 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "domingos2000mining"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Mondrian Forest
\series default
:
 A highly efficient adaptation of forest tree ensembles for online learning 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lakshminarayanan2014mondrian"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Locally Weighted Projection Regression (LWPR)
\series default
:
 The most direct competitor of kCELL as it uses a similar local linear model spatialization but employs different learning logic 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "vijayakumar2000locally"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To implement Adaptive Hoeffding Trees,
 Mondrian Forest,
 Linear Regression with Adam optimizer and kNN,
 the python library 
\emph on
RiverML
\emph default
 is used 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "montiel2021river"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 For LWPR,
 the original python C-bindings are used.
 Performing an exhaustive hyperparameter optimization on the whole dataset is impracticable because it is too computationally heavy.
 For this reason,
 hyperparameter optimization was conducted via grid search on the first 5000 steps of the stream to minimize the Area Under the Curve (AUC) of the prequential error.
 The 
\emph on
prequential error
\emph default
 corresponds to the error measured for a new point of the data stream before learning on it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The performance of the models is evaluated through adaptation speed,
 measured by prequential error,
 and accuracy measured by the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) and Mean Absolute Error (MAE) on the fixed test set.
\end_layout

