\begin_layout Paragraph
Controlled Population Growth in kCELL
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The efficiency of the self-organization process is further analyzed by monitoring the evolution of the population of local expert agents.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:kCELL-number-of-Agents"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 tracks the number of agents in the ensemble throughout the learning process compared to a theoretical maximum.
 The theoretical maximum is computed from the minimum number of points 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{ini}}$
\end_inset

 needed for the system to be able to create an agent.
 In kCELL,
 an agent can be created,
 at most,
 every 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{ini}}$
\end_inset

 steps because the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{B}_{\text{ini}}$
\end_inset

 buffer is cleared at each new agent creation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The curve representing the number of agents remains strictly below the theoretical maximum.
 Furthermore,
 the gap between the actual number of agents and this theoretical maximum increases over time.
 The growth rate of the population of local expert agents is visibly flattening as more data is processed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This behavior demonstrates that kCELL is not systematically creating new models.
 Instead the model successfully evaluates its current knowledge map through the areas of expertise of agents,
 triggering the 
\series bold
Incompetence
\series default
 rule only when a significant gap in the input space is identified.
 The flattening of the curve of the number of agent indicates a convergence toward a stable population.
 This highlights the effectiveness of the population management mechanism (
\series bold
Uselessness
\series default
 rule) which prunes redundant or underperforming agents by evaluating their social confidence.
\end_layout

