\begin_layout Standard
These two environments were chosen to contrast a low-dimensional setting (Inverted Pendulum) with a higher-dimensional one (Hopper).
 Mahalanobis distances become larger the higher the number of dimensions.
 Thus,
 we want to study the impact of increasing dimensionality on the representativeness of the spatialization of agents in kCELL which was one of the main limitations previously identified for oCELL (cf.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Limitations"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the context of this experiment the task to solve is a forward modeling task.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\series bold
Forward dynamics modeling
\series default
 is the construction of a model that maps states (such as positions and velocities) and actions to future state trajectories.
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "Forward Dynamics"
description "Forward dynamics modeling is the construction of a model that maps states (such as positions and velocities) and actions to future state trajectories."
literal "true"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The model learns online from a data stream to estimate the transition function 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 of the controlled dynamical system,
 defined by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
s_{t+1}=f\left(s_{t},u_{t}\right)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the current state of the dynamical system,
 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t+1}$
\end_inset

 the next state,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the applied action,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To simulate non-stationarity,
 three datasets 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{1},\mathcal{D}_{2},\mathcal{D}_{3}$
