\begin_layout Standard
Each dataset 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is split into a training set 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}^{\text{train}}$
\end_inset

 and a held-out test set 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}^{\text{test}}$
\end_inset

.
 Throughout the learning process,
 the training datasets are streamed in a sequential order (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{1}^{\text{train}}$
\end_inset


\emph on
 
\begin_inset Formula $\rightarrow$
\end_inset


\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{2}^{\text{train}}$
\end_inset


\emph on
 
\begin_inset Formula $\rightarrow$
\end_inset

 
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{3}^{\text{train}}$
\end_inset

) to emulate abrupt changes in the underlying system dynamics (i.e.
 non-stationarities).
 The learned models are regularly evaluated against all three test datasets 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \mathcal{D}_{j}^{\text{test}}\right\} _{j=1}^{3}$
\end_inset

.
 By comparing the index 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 of the current training set 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}^{\text{train}}$
\end_inset

 with the index 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 of the test set 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{j}^{\text{test}}$
\end_inset

,
 we assess:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Performance
\series default
 on the currently observed dynamics (i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $i=j$
\end_inset

);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Retention
\series default
 or 
\series bold
Forgetting 
\series default
of previously encountered dynamics (i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $i>j$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

