\begin_layout Paragraph
Structural Evolution of Agent Population
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To better understand these adaptation patterns,
 the evolution of the local expert agent population displayed in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Evolution-of-nb-agents"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 is analyzed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Inverted Pendulum
\series default
:
 Each change in dynamics (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}^{\text{train}}\rightarrow\mathcal{D}_{i+1}^{\text{train}}$
\end_inset

) induces a short-term burst in the number of agents following by a reduction of this number and a stabilization.
 This indicates a restructuration of the population.
 New dynamics trigger agent creation while the confidence-based mechanism prunes older agents that are no longer relevant to the current dynamics (cf.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Visualization-of-nb-agents-invertedpendulum"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Hopper
\series default
:
 The number of agents grows monotonically.
 Distinct growth regimes are observed for each gravity value.
 The growth is logarithmic for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{1}^{\text{train}}$
\end_inset

(
\emph on
red area
\emph default
),
 becomes linear for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{2}^{\text{train}}$
\end_inset

(
\emph on
yellow area
\emph default
),
 and then stabilizes around 20000 steps for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{3}^{\text{train}}$
\end_inset

(
\emph on
green area
\emph default
).
 This suggests an accumulation of local expert agents rather than a replacement of the existing population (cf.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Visualization-of-nb-agents-hopper"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
We analyze the evolution of agent population during training illustrated in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Evolution-of-nb-agents"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 As previously hinted by MAE analysis,
 we notice contrasting dynamics in the population evolution during learning between the two environments.
 In Inverted Pendulum (cf.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Visualization-of-nb-agents-invertedpendulum"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 each gravity change induces a short-term surge in the number of agents,
 followed by a reduction and stabilization around a nominal value.
 This indicates a restructuring of the population,
 where novelty triggers agent creation and confidence-based mechanisms triggers destruction to eliminate less relevant agents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In Hopper (cf.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Visualization-of-nb-agents-hopper"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 the number of agents grows monotonically throughout training.
 Distinct growth regimes are observed for each gravity value with first a logaritmic growth (
\begin_inset Formula $g=9.81$
\end_inset

),
 second a seemingly linear growth (
\begin_inset Formula $g=4$
\end_inset

) and third a slight logarithmic growth (
\begin_inset Formula $g=20$
\end_inset

) with stabilization around 20k steps.
 This accumulation of agents suggests a continuous detection of local dynamics without systematic removal of existing agents.
\end_layout

