\begin_layout Paragraph
Knowledge Retention Behavior Analysis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike the Inverted Pendulum environment,
 for the higher-dimensional Hopper environment,
 a 
\series bold
knowledge retention
\series default
 effect has been identified (cf.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Visualization-of-mae-dynamic-changes-hopper"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) during the learning process.
 This effect can be attributed to the
\series bold
 agent persistence
\series default
 within the kCELL model (cf.
 Figures 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Visualization-of-nb-agents-hopper"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Visualization-of-age-hopper"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 which may be driven by two non-exclusive mechanisms:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Adaptive Knowledge Reuse
\series default
:
 Agents trained on earlier datasets 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}^{\text{train}}$
\end_inset

 might retain partial competence in overlapping regions of the state-action space (i.e.
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\,i\neq j$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}^{\text{train}}\cap\mathcal{D}_{j}^{\text{train}}\neq\emptyset$
\end_inset

),
 allowing them to adapt incrementally as new data arrives.
 In this scenario,
 population growth is mainly driven by the additional modeling complexity introduced by the change in dynamics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Spatial Separation
\series default
:
 High-dimensional effects may stretch the Mahalanobis distance,
 yielding almost zero activation values for older agents when changing dynamics,
 preventing both meaningful adaptation and confidence-based degradation (as older agents are never updated again).
 In this case,
 older agents become frozen in previously visited regions of the state-action space that do not overlap with newly discovered ones (i.e 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\,i\neq j,\,\mathcal{D}_{i}^{\text{train}}\cap\mathcal{D}_{j}^{\text{train}}=\emptyset$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To disambiguate these two mechanisms,
 we analyze the activation patterns of the final agent population across the entire training sequence (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{1}^{\text{train}}\rightarrow\mathcal{D}_{2}^{\text{train}}\rightarrow\mathcal{D}_{3}^{\text{train}}$
\end_inset

).
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Heatmap-of-activations-hopper"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 presents a heatmap of these activation values,
 where the 
\emph on
x
\emph default
-axis represents the agents sorted by age (from oldest to youngest) and the 
\emph on
y
\emph default
-axis represents the chronological stream of training data (aggregated into bins).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The heatmap reveals a clear block-diagonal structure which provides strong evidence in favor of a 
\series bold
Spatial Separation
\series default
 mechanism.
 Agents remain highly activated for data points belonging to the specific dataset on which they were originally created and trained.
 Indeed,
 there are very few cross-dataset activations:
 for instance,
 agents activated by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{1}$
\end_inset

 samples no not show significant activation values for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{2}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{3}$
\end_inset

 samples.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This lack of overlap suggests that the observed knowledge retention for the Hopper environment is not due to continuous adaptation of the ensemble of local expert agents,
 but rather to the spatial separation of several subsets of agents.
 Each change in dynamics induces the creation of a new subpopulation of agents that coexists with the previous ones without replacing them.
\end_layout

