\begin_layout Paragraph
Discussion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The experimental results presented in this section demonstrate that the spatial organization of local expert agents in kCELL can be directly exploited during trajectory optimization to improve the reliability of the controller.
 Incorporating a distance-based regularization term into the MPC cost effectively bias the optimizer toward the regions of the state space where the learned dynamics are supported by training data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The results show that this regularization mechanism facilitates 
\series bold
smarter model usage rather than model improvement
\series default
.
 The observed reduction in MAE and the attenuation of compounding errors without any modification of the frozen kCELL model indicate that kCELL introspective properties can be leveraged to inform an optimizer to distinguish between high-confidence interpolations and risky extrapolations.
 This makes the controller more reliable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Furthermore,
 these results provide empirical support for the Decreasing Knowledge assumption at the core of kCELL algorithm (cf.
 Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "assumption:Decreasing-Knowledge-Assumption"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 A correlation between agent activation and reduced prediction error can be observed through the effects of the regularization mechanism.
 This observation is consistent with the results presented in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:kCELL-Online-Stationary-Modeling"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and further reinforces the assumption that local linear models become increasingly reliable near their center.
 The regularization mechanism thus transforms this assumption into a control-relevant advantage.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 this improved reliability comes at the cost of task optimality.
 The identified safety-performance trade-off reveals that as 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 increases,
 the controller 
\series bold
increasingly prioritizes known states over minimizing the task cost
\series default
.
 The nonlinear nature of the Pareto frontier illustrated in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Pareto-Error-vs-Cost"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 seems promising,
 as it suggests that substantial gains in predictive reliability could be achieved for a relatively minor increase in cumulative cost.
 The regularization mechanism acts as a soft feasibility constraint on the validity of the model,
 considering that unknown regions are either unfeasible or dangerous.
 This is similar to trust-region methods in nonlinear optimization 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "conn2000trust,schulman2015trust"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Limitations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While these experimental results demonstrate the potential of introspection-driven conservatism to improve the reliability of a MPC controller,
 several limitations remain.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Although the distance-based measure correlates strongly with accuracy,
 it remains a heuristic proxy for epistemic uncertainty rather than a calibrated probability.
 Consequently,
 this approach should be viewed as a risk-reduction mechanism rather than a formal safety guarantee.
 The efficiency of the regularization is fundamentally dependent on the quality of the local expert agent distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This case study has leveraged a low-dimensional system (the Pendulum) where Mahalanobis distances remain numerically meaningful.
 In higher-dimensional state-action spaces,
 agent coverage naturally becomes sparser.
 The distance to the nearest agent may become less informative due to the curse of dimensionality.
 The efficiency of this regularization in more complex high-dimensional systems remains a subject for further investigation.
 In addition,
 the effect of the sparsity of the population of agents on the conservative regularization behavior has not been studied.
 Training multiple models with varying agent densities would be necessary to better characterize how coverage quality impacts the reliability gains.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The decision to spatialize agents solely in the state space was a task-specific choice made to accelerate population stabilization during the learning phase of the frozen kCELL model.
 This choice limits the expressiveness of agents regarding actions.
 While this design choice is appropriate for the Pendulum,
 it may not generalize to more complex high-dimensional dynamical systems.
 In such cases,
 regularization in joint state-action space could be required.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 the proposed approach discourages extrapolation into unknown regions but does not provide rigorous barrier certificates or Lyapunov stability guarantees required for safety-critical certification.
 Given the transparency of the inner workings of kCELL models,
 this could represent a promising research area for future work.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Synthesis and Conclusion
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Synthesis-and-Conclusion-Control"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This chapter has explored the practical integration of a kCELL predictive model into a model-based controller through the MPC framework.
 It has bridged the gap between the theoretical foundations laid out with the CELL formalism in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Context-Ensemble-Local"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and the concrete challenges of control where no prior knowledge of the dynamics is available.
 By leveraging the emerging structure of the ensemble of local expert agents of kCELL,
 we demonstrated how this type of learning model can use its own internal state to improve both data collection (
\series bold
exploration
\series default
) and control reliability (
\series bold
exploitation
\series default
).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Exploration-MPC"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 has addressed the exploration challenge in unknown environments.
 Two introspection metrics derived from a continuously learning kCELL model have been introduced:
 
\series bold
distance-based expertise
\series default
 and local 
\series bold
model disagreement
\series default
.
 The experimental results obtained on the Pendulum task have shown that incorporating these signals into the MPC cost allows the controller to systematically discover complex dynamics that uninformed random strategies fail to reach.
 This confirms that kCELL's social multi-agent structure provides a reliable mechanism for autonomous unsupervised data acquisition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Conservative-MPC"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 has tackled the reliability challenge of learned models during exploitation.
 The distance-based expertise signal has been repurposed as a conservative regularization term used to mitigate the error compounding effect.
 It represents a major challenge in model-based control with learned dynamics.
 By biasing the optimizer within MPC,
 toward existing kCELL agents,
 we have demonstrated a significant reduction in perception errors between the ground truth and the predictive model.
 This contribution highlights the ability of kCELL to provide a meaningful proxy for epistemic uncertainty (derived from the spatial organization of local expert agents) that can be integrated into a gradient-based optimization loop (e.g.
 with SQP).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Overall,
 the conclusions of this chapter support the continuous local expertise assumption at the core of kCELL's design.
 We have shown that the spatial organization of kCELL agents represents a functional asset that contributes to a 
\emph on
smarter model usage
\emph default
.
 The identified trade-off between task performance and predictive reliability provides a promising lead for designing safer controllers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Despite these interesting and promising results,
 significant research gaps remain.
 Indeed,
 due to the way the agents are spatialized in kCELL,
 it suffers from the curse of dimensionality,
 hurting its scaling capabilities in higher dimensional input spaces because distances are stretching and the notion of neighborhood becomes meaningless.
 This represents a crucial research direction for learning systems based on an ensemble of local learning agents governed by social rules.
 We suggest that future works should draw inspiration from how LWPR handles an increasing number of input dimension through Partial Least Squares (PLS) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "vijayakumar2000locally"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 PLS is a method that projects the data onto a lower-dimensional latent subspace capturing the directions of greatest covariance between inputs and outputs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Moreover,
 despite its ability to quantify epistemic uncertainty,
 kCELL lacks predictive uncertainty estimation.
 In safe control with Gaussian Processes (GP) for example,
 predictive uncertainty can be leveraged in the optimization process to robustify control by taking into account the propagation of uncertainties along predicted trajectories.
 To this end,
 Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Scalable-Non-Linear-CELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 explores the modularity of the CELL formalism by proposing a novel instantiation,
 gpCELL,
 that leverages GPs as the internal model of local expert agents to introduce principled predictive uncertainty quantification.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Heterogeneous nonlinear CELL
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "chap:Scalable-Non-Linear-CELL"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the previous chapters,
 the CELL (Context Ensemble Local Learning) formalism has been established as a robust framework for designing online learning algorithms.
 Specifically Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Context-Ensemble-Local"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 introduced the first instantiations of this formalism and demonstrated their ability to maintain a stable and informative spatialized representation of knowledge.
 Building upon these foundations,
 Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Solving-Control-Tasks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 has focused on the integration of these learning models into a Model Predictive Control (MPC) pipeline.
 This integration has highlighted how the introspective properties emerging from the structural transparency of the ensemble of local expert agents,
 could be used to improve the reliability of the predictive model in an optimization-based control problem,
 or to guide exploration in an unknown environment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This chapter moves away from control applications and gets back to the underlying predictive model layer to explore a core property of the CELL formalism:
 
\series bold
model-agnosticity
\series default
.
 Because the spatial organization and the local learning layers are decoupled,
 the agents within an ensemble can theoretically use a wide range of model classes.
 Exploiting this modularity allows for the design of heterogeneous populations.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
In the context of this thesis,
 a 
\series bold
heterogeneous ensemble of local expert agents
\series default
 denotes a population of agents whose internal representational capacities vary across the input space,
 adapting to the complexity of the local training distribution (as illustrated in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Illustration-Heterogeneous"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement document
alignment document
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename assets/Heterogeneous_Agents.pdf
	lyxscale 50
	width 70col%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Heterogeneous ensemble of local expert agents.
 Each
\emph on
 blue
\emph default
 
\emph on
rectangle
\emph default
 corresponds to the area of expertise of one local expert agent.
 Each agent has a different type of internal model (
\begin_inset Formula $f_{1},\,f_{2},\,f_{3}$
\end_inset

),
 as illustrated by the three plots on top of the diagram.
 The 
\emph on
red dots
\emph default
 correspond to the local training points used for training.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:Illustration-Heterogeneous"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Traditionally,
 in multi-agent systems,
 heterogeneity refers to a mixture of distinct agent classes.
 For example,
 some agents would employ simple linear regressions while others would use high-order polynomials or neural networks.
 While such an approach offers theoretical flexibility,
 it introduces a significant meta-learning challenge:
 the model must decide a priori or through complex heuristics which model class is best suited for a specific locality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To bypass the need for manual model selection while retaining the benefits of diverse local knowledge representation,
 we propose a novel instantiation of CELL.
 This instantiation leverages Gaussian Processes (GP) as the internal model for each agent and is called gpCELL.
 By using the GPs' ability to act as universal function approximators,
 the expressive power of each agent is not fixed,
 but emerges from its local training distribution.
 This approach not only allows the agent to naturally adapt to varying local data complexities but also introduces an estimation of predictive uncertainty.
 This predictive uncertainty is complementary to the epistemic uncertainty derived from the spatial organization of local expert agents (cf.
 Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Context-Ensemble-Local"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The contributions presented in this chapter are threefold:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Scalable Online Gaussian Processes
\series default
:
 We introduce gpCELL,
 a novel CELL instantiation that bypasses the 
\begin_inset Formula $O\left(n^{3}\right)$
\end_inset

 computational bottleneck of global GPs through a horizontally scaling,
 distributed architecture.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Uncertainty Quantification in CELL
\series default
:
 The integration of GPs in a CELL instantiation enables a more rigorous estimation of predictive uncertainty.
 We demonstrate through an online modeling task that this uncertainty is empirically well-calibrated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Heterogeneous Agent Design
\series default
:
 Through model selection,
 gpCELL allows each agent to independently adapt its complexity to the local data manifold.
 Thus,
 gpCELL represents a step towards the design of learning systems that take advantage of a heterogeneous ensemble of local expert agents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Gaussian Process Regression
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Gaussian-Process-Regression"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Gaussian Process Regression (GPR) is a Bayesian non-parametric framework used for supervised learning.
 Unlike traditional regression models that estimate fixed parameters for a specific function,
 GPR provides a 
\series bold
probabilistic distribution over possible functions
\series default
 that match the observed data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
GPR is widely used for modeling complex stochastic phenomena where uncertainty quantification is as critical as the prediction itself,
 such as in signal processing 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "perez2013gaussian"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 in financial modeling 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "gonzalvez2019financial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 or in dynamics modeling for safe control 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "berkenkamp2015safe"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One of the main advantages of Gaussian Processes (GPs) is that,
 with an appropriate choice of kernel (e.g.
 RBF kernel),
 they can represent arbitrarily complex continuous functions 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "williams2006gaussian"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 capturing any variation regime from highly irregular trajectories to smooth differentiable paths.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In GPR,
 the underlying function 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 is assumed to follow a GP prior which is defined by a function 
\begin_inset Formula $m\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 (often assumed to be 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 or constant) and a covariance kernel 
\begin_inset Formula $K\left(x,x^{\prime}\right)$
\end_inset

 such as:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f\left(x\right)\sim\mathcal{GP}\left(m\left(x\right),K\left(x,x^{\prime}\right)\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

For a supervised learning task with training inputs 
\begin_inset Formula $X\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}$
\end_inset

 and noisy observations 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in\mathbb{R}^{n}$
\end_inset

,
 assuming a zero mean prior,
 the joint distribution of both the training data and the prediction 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(x_{*}\right)$
\end_inset

 at a new test point 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{*}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$
\end_inset

,
 is a multivariate Gaussian vector:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left(\begin{array}{c}
y\\
f\left(x_{*}\right)
\end{array}\right)\sim\mathcal{N}\left(0,\left(\begin{array}{cc}
K\left(X,X\right)+\sigma_{n}^{2}I & K\left(X,x_{*}\right)\\
K\left(x_{*},X\right) & K\left(x_{*},x_{*}\right)
\end{array}\right)\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{n}^{2}$
\end_inset

 represents the observation noise variance.
 The prediction is performed by conditioning this joint distribution on the observed data 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
 This results in a posterior distribution for 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(x_{*}\right)$
\end_inset

 defined by:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Predictive Mean
\series default
:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mu_{*}=K\left(x_{*},X\right)\left[K\left(X,X\right)+\sigma_{n}^{2}I\right]^{-1}y
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Predictive Variance
\series default
:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sigma_{*}^{2}=K\left(x_{*},x_{*}\right)-K\left(x_{*},X\right)\left[K\left(X,X\right)+\sigma_{n}^{2}I\right]^{-1}K\left(X,x_{*}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Learning in GPR does not involve finding specific weights but rather 
\series bold
optimizing the hyperparameters
\series default
 of the kernel 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

.
 This is usually achieved by maximizing the 
\series bold
log marginal likelihood
\series default
,
 which balances the model's fit to the training data against its complexity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Despite their flexibility,
 GPs have significant constraints:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Computational Complexity
\series default
:
 The inversion of the covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[K\left(X,X\right)+\sigma_{n}^{2}I\right]$
\end_inset

 has a complexity in 
\begin_inset Formula $O\left(n^{3}\right)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 the number of training data which is impractical for large datasets and for online learning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Memory Usage
\series default
:
 Storing the covariance matrix requires 
\begin_inset Formula $O\left(n^{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 space.
 This makes GPR hard to scale to large datasets without using approximation methods (like Sparse Gaussian Processes 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bauer2016understanding"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Local Expert Agents
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Local-Expert-Agents-gpCELL"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The previous section has presented the Gaussian Process Regression (GPR) and it has emphasized on the 
\begin_inset Formula $O\left(n^{3}\right)$
\end_inset

 computational complexity bottleneck that limits its application on large datasets or for online learning.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To address these limitations,
 gpCELL applies the principle of 
\series bold
horizontal scaling
\series default
:
 rather than maintaining a single global GP,
 the system distributes the modeling task across an ensemble of local expert agents,
 each containing a smaller GP.
 As a result,
 each GP learns with fewer data points and can be refitted when needed in a reasonable amount of time.
 This section details the tripartite structure of the local expert agents in gpCELL.
 This tripartite structure follows the architectural principles of the CELL formalism.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each agent in gpCELL is a local expert that manages a GP and is defined as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{A}_{i}=\left\{ \phi_{i},f_{i},\mathcal{M}_{i}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

 denotes its activation function,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

 its internal model,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

 its internal state.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
The spatialization logic of kCELL is retained in gpCELL.

\series default
 Each agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents its area of expertise through a mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$
\end_inset

 and a covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}$
\end_inset

.
 The activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\mapsto\left]0,1\right]$
\end_inset

 for a given input 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$
\end_inset

 is given by the RBF kernel value
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\phi_{i}\left(x\right)=\exp\left(-\frac{d_{M}\left(\mathcal{A}_{i},x\right)^{2}}{2l^{2}}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 the lengthscale of the kernel and 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{M}\left(\mathcal{A}_{i},x\right)$
\end_inset

 a measure of similarity between an agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and an input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{M}\left(\mathcal{A}_{i},x\right)$
\end_inset

 is the Mahalanobis distance as in kCELL,
 and it allows gpCELL to remain compatible with the 
\series bold
same spatial gating mechanism as kCELL
\series default
 (cf.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:kCELL-Context-Agents"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike the linear models used in oCELL or kCELL,
 each agent in gpCELL maintains a 
\series bold
local GP as the internal model 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset


\series default
.
 By using a GP,
 an agent can provide not only a mean prediction 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{*,i}$
\end_inset

 but also a predictive variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{*,i}^{2}$
\end_inset

 representing the uncertainty about the prediction for a given input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 The internal model 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

 is defined as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f_{i}\left(x\right)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
\mu_{*,i}\\
\sigma_{*,i}
\end{array}\right)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
K\left(x,X_{i,\text{train}}\right)\left[K\left(X_{i,\text{train}},X_{i,\text{train}}\right)+\sigma_{n}^{2}I\right]^{-1}y_{i,\text{train}}\\
K\left(x,x\right)-K\left(x,X_{i,\text{train}}\right)\left[K\left(X_{i,\text{train}},X_{i,\text{train}}\right)+\sigma_{n}^{2}I\right]^{-1}K\left(X_{i,\text{train}},x\right)
\end{array}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i,\text{train}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i,\text{train}}$
\end_inset

 correspond to the feature-target dataset of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 stored in its internal state 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The internal state 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}_{i}$
\end_inset

 tracks a confidence value 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{C}_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}\in\left[-1,1\right]$
\end_inset

.
 This confidence value is an exponential moving average of received social feedback that is described in detail in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:kCELL-Learning-Rules"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 It also maintains an internal feature-target dataset 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

 used to retrain the internal model 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 This dataset contains the features matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i,\text{train}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{\text{retained}}\times d}$
\end_inset

 and the target matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i,\text{train}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n_{\text{retained}}}$
\end_inset

.
 This dataset is managed dynamically by the agent and will be presented in the remainder of this section.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
GP predictions revert to the specified prior mean function when inputs are far from the training data support,
 because the covariance kernel decays with distance.
 Consequently,
 it can lead to highly unreliable predictions and underestimation of the predictive variance 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "williams2006gaussian"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The aggregation function used to build the global prediction of the ensemble of GP-based local expert agents must take this phenomenon into consideration to avoid taking into account the proposition of an unreliable agent that would pollute the final prediction.
 To ensure reliability of predictions,
 gpCELL use a 
\series bold
winner-take-all strategy
\series default
 for global prediction based on predictive variance.
 For a given input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 the global prediction 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}$
\end_inset

 is provided by the agent within the set of neighbor agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

 that reports the minimum predictive variance:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{y}=f_{j}\left(x\right)\,\,\text{where}\,\,j=\arg\min_{i\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}}\left(\sigma_{*,i}^{2}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This aggregation function prioritizes the agent that is the most certain about the local structure of the data.
 In other words,
 the selected agent is the one whose internal representation of the underlying function is best aligned with the input 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Model and Shape Update
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In gpCELL,
 the update of the internal model 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

 and the shape of the area of expertise (
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{i}$
\end_inset

) are intrinsically linked.
 Since the shape of the agent is obtained from the distribution of points stored in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and used to retrain 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 updating 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

 automatically updates the area of expertise of the agent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When a new point 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(x_{new},y_{new}\right)$
\end_inset

 is processed,
 the agent must decide if or how this information should be included to its internal model.
 This is treated as a local model selection problem.
 The agent evaluates several possible configurations:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Nominal
\series default
:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

 remains unchanged and the current internal model is kept without modification.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Substitution
\series default
 
\series bold
of point 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset


\series default
:
 The new point replaces an existing point 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(x_{j},y_{j}\right)$
\end_inset

 contained in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Augmentation
\series default
:
 The new point is added to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each configuration corresponds to a configuration of the internal dataset 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and by extension,
 to a resulting internal model 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 The number of configurations 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{\text{configurations}}$
\end_inset

 is equal to:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
n_{\text{configurations}}=\underbrace{1}_{\text{Nominal}}+\underbrace{\left|\mathcal{D}_{i}\right|}_{\text{Subtitutions}}+\underbrace{1}_{\text{Augmentation}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To maintain computational efficiency for large memories,
 instead of evaluating all possible substitution possibilities by building a new model for each,
 heuristics can be used to select only one substitution configuration.
 Examples of heuristics that can be used in this context are given below:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Random Selection
\series default
:
 A substitution configuration is selected randomly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Influence-Based Selection
\series default
:
 This heuristic aims to select the point that contributes least to the final prediction of the GP.
 Points that are highly correlated within the agent's memory tend to have low influence.
 This means that removing them would have only a minimal impact on the model's predictive performance.
 The influence is estimated from the inverse of the covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

.
 Smaller diagonal entries of 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 indicate less influential points.
 The less influential point 
\begin_inset Formula $j^{*}$
\end_inset

 is obtained by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
j^{*}=\arg\min_{j\in X_{i,\text{train}}}\left[K^{-1}\right]_{jj}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using such a heuristic reduces the number of evaluated configurations to 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
n_{\text{configurations}}=3
\]

\end_inset

 regardless of the memory size (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To choose the optimal configurations,
 the agent computes a variant of the Akaike Information Criterion (AIC) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "akaike2003new"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for each possibility:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
AIC\left(x_{new},y_{new}\right)=\alpha\left|\mathcal{D}_{i}\right|-2\ln\left(\hat{L}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 is the weight of the number of points 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\mathcal{D}_{i}\right|$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\mathcal{D}_{i}\right|$
\end_inset

 corresponds to the number of parameters representing the non-parametric complexity of the model,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\ln\left(\hat{L}\right)$
\end_inset

 is the log-marginal likelihood of the obtained GP given the training data contained in
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By selecting the configuration that minimizes the AIC,
 the agent performs a greedy online trade-off between predictive accuracy and model parsimony.
 When a new configuration is chosen,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is updated accordingly and the internal GP is retrained.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

 has changed,
 the shape of the area of expertise (
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{i}$
\end_inset

) is automatically updated based on the empirical mean and covariance of the new 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i,\text{train}}$
\end_inset

.
 Finally,
 for stability reasons and to have a bounded complexity,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

 should have a maximum size 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{max}$
\end_inset

 (beyond which point can only be swapped and not added) and a minimum size analogous to the 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{ini}$
\end_inset

 in kCELL.
 We clarify that each point beyond 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{max}$
\end_inset

 can only be swapped and not added.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Computational Complexity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Considering only one neighbor update,
 the use of a heuristic that limits the number of GP fits in the model selection process,
 and a 
\begin_inset Formula $O\left(dN\right)$
\end_inset

 complexity for selecting neighbor agents,
 the complexity of an update step in gpCELL becomes 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
O\left(\underbrace{dN}_{\text{neighbor search}}\times\underbrace{3IN_{max}^{3}}_{\text{evaluate all config.}}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 is the number of iteration required to find the best GP parameters,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is the number of agents.
 As demonstrated in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Solving-Control-Tasks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 neighbor selection is embarrassingly parallel.
 Thus,
 the complexity of this operation can be greatly reduced by using GPU parallelization.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Considering an online learning context where a classical GP is fitted on the whole dataset at each step,
 the computational complexity of an update is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
O\left(In^{3}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{max}\ll n$
\end_inset

,
 gpCELL is more practical because it relies on a bounded cubic term,
 unlike the classical GP whose cubic term is unbounded (scaling with the number of training data).
 Although this is a simplified example,
 since methods like Sparse GPs 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bauer2016understanding"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 aim to reduce the update complexity,
 it reveals the computational stability of gpCELL.
 The complexity of gpCELL scales with the number of agents rather than the number of training points.
 If the embarrassingly parallel nature of neighbor search is exploited properly,
 a close-to-bounded update complexity could theoretically be reached.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Example of Single Agent Fit
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To visualize the learning result for a single local expert agent,
 we use a 1-dimensional example.
 A small synthetic dataset is generated from the function 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(x\right)=\sin\left(x\right)+\epsilon\mathcal{N}\left(0,1\right)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 the noise scaling factor that we set to 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon=10^{-2}$
\end_inset

.
 Online learning is simulated by feeding the feature-target tuples (
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new},y_{new}$
\end_inset

) one at a time to the agent.
 Each point is only seen once and the agent is configured with an initial memory size of 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{ini}=4$
\end_inset

,
 a parameter weight 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=1$
\end_inset

 and an RBF kernel with a constant prior mean 
\begin_inset Formula $m\left(x\right)=0$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Illustration-of-GP-Agent-Learning"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 illustrates the result of learning a single agent on this dataset.
 Several observations about the agent behavior can be made:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Local Anchoring
\series default
:
 The agent does not attempt to cover the entire domain that spans over 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\left[-6,6\right]$
\end_inset

.
 It maintains its area of expertise within a localized region.
 It demonstrates that the model-selection with AIC prevents the model from over-stretching to include points that would require excessive model complexity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Dynamic Memory Management
\series default
:
 The final set of retained points (represented as 
\emph on
red
\emph default
 dots) is larger than the minimal size 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{ini}=4$
\end_inset

 required to create the agent.
 Indeed,
 5 points have been retained.
 However,
 the set of retained points remains sparse over the area of expertise.
 This means that the agent has used both 
\series bold
substitutions
\series default
 and 
\series bold
augmentations
\series default
 to optimize its internal representation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Complexity-Accuracy Trade-off
\series default
:
 The sparsity of the retained points suggests that the agent has selected only the most informative points for obtaining a high-likelihood model.
 This results in a parsimonious model that captures the local curvature of the underlying function.
\end_layout

