\begin_layout Section
Learning Rules
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Learning-Rules-gpCELL"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The previous section detailed the tripartite structure of gpCELL agents.
 It has focused on how GP-based local expert agents manage their local complexity through AIC model selection.
 This section describes the social learning rules that govern the behavior of the ensemble of local expert agents.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In gpCELL the reliability of an agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is tracked via its running confidence 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{C}_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}\in\left[-1,1\right]$
\end_inset

.
 This confidence is updated using an exponential moving average of normalized social feedbacks.
 The agent is compared to a predictive baseline 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{base}$
\end_inset

,
 that is derived from the weighted contributions of other experts which are close within the ensemble.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Social Baseline Definition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The baseline 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{base}$
\end_inset

 is defined as a weighted average of the predictions 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{*,i}$
\end_inset

 (the predictive means) from a specific set of agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{social}}$
\end_inset

.
 The contribution of each agent is proportional to its activation value 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}\left(x_{new}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 representing its expertise:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{base}=\sum_{i\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{social}}}w_{i}\left(x_{new}\right)\mu_{*,i}\,\,\text{with}\,\,w_{i}\left(x_{new}\right)=\frac{\phi_{i}}{\sum_{j\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{social}}}\phi_{j}\left(x_{new}\right)}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The composition of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{social}}$
\end_inset

 depends on the local social context:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Neighbor Feedback
\series default
 (
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{neighbors}}>1$
\end_inset

):
 When several agents are neighbors to the given input 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{social}}$
\end_inset

 contains the neighbor agents,
 excluding 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 such as:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{D}_{\text{social}}=\mathcal{D}_{\text{neighbors}}\backslash\left\{ \mathcal{A}_{i}\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Closest Feedback
\series default
 (
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{neighbors}}=1$
\end_inset

):
 When there is only one neighbor to the given input 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

,
 its prediction is compared against the set of the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-closest agents 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{k\text{-closest}}$
\end_inset

,
 excluding itself such as :
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{D}_{\text{social}}=\mathcal{D}_{k\text{-closest}}\backslash\left\{ \mathcal{A}_{i}\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This allows the prediction of the neighbor agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 to be benchmarked against a default broader baseline.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The relative performance of agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is evaluated as the fractional reduction in error:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}=\frac{\left|y_{base}-y_{new}\right|-\left|\hat{y}-y_{new}\right|}{\left|y_{base}-y_{new}\right|}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{new}$
\end_inset

 is the ground truth and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}$
\end_inset

 is the weighted average of predictions of agents within 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{\text{social}}\cup\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 such as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{y}=\sum_{j\in\left(\mathcal{D}_{\text{social}}\cup\mathcal{A}_{i}\right)}w_{j}\left(x_{new}\right)\mu_{*,j}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}>0$
\end_inset

 indicates the agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a strong expert that improves the collective prediction,
 while 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}<0$
\end_inset

 identifies a weak expert whose internal model diverges from the social consensus.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To ensure the stability of the running confidence 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{C}_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}$
\end_inset

 against outliers,
 the raw feedback is normalized into an instantaneous confidence 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}\in\left[-1,1\right]$
\end_inset

 using a hyperbolic tangent function such as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
c_{i}=\tanh\left(k_{\text{steepness}}\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{\text{steepness}}$
\end_inset

 is a hyperparameter representing the steepness of the normalization curve.
 This ensures that extremely large relative errors do not cause catastrophic drops in confidence.
 The running confidence is then updated as an exponential moving average of the instantaneous confidence values:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\bar{C}_{\mathcal{A}_{i}|t+1}=\left(1-\lambda\right)\bar{C}_{\mathcal{A}_{i}|t}+\lambda c_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 is the smoothing factor.
 This value is used as the primary metric for pruning bad agents in the ensemble.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The learning rules in gpCELL translate the social context and the result of the AIC model selection into discrete actions and meta-actions.
 The learning rules are detailed in the following paragraphs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Inaccuracy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{neighbors}}\geq1$
\end_inset

,
 the closest agent performs its AIC-based selection.
 If the new point is informative enough to be included in the internal dataset 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{D}_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 the internal GP model and the shape of its area of expertise are updated (
\series bold
Model and Shape Update
\series default
).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Incompetence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{neighbors}}=0$
\end_inset

,
 the system identifies a gap in the local representation of the function to be approximated.
 The closest agent in the ensemble attempts to ingest the new feature-target tuple (
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new},y_{new}$
\end_inset

) by evaluating the AIC configurations.
 If the AIC selection results in keeping the 
\series bold
nominal
\series default
 configuration,
 it means that an update is likely to corrupt its internal representation,
 degrading the complexity-accuracy trade-off.
 In this case,
 an 
\series bold
Agent Creation
\series default
 meta-action is triggered to spawn a new agent into the ensemble.
 This new agent is initialized from the 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{ini}$
\end_inset

 most recent feature-target tuples encountered in the data stream.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Uselessness
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If an agent's running confidence 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{C}_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}$
\end_inset

 falls below a survival threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{\text{confidence}}$
\end_inset

,
 the agent is too inaccurate relative to the social consensus and is removed from the ensemble.
 This prevents uncontrolled population growth.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

