\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Learning rules of gpCELL.
 Each line corresponds to a specific learning rule defined by:
 1) a social context expressed as a condition on the number 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{neighbors}}$
\end_inset

 of local experts around a new input 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

;
 2) a condition on the outcome of the model selection process (nominal,
 substitution,
 augmentation) or on the running confidence 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{C}_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}$
\end_inset

;
 3) a target set of agents that will be affected by the rule;
 and 4) the actions or meta-actions to perform on the agents of the targeted set.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "tab:gpCELL-Learning-Rules"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 implementation-wise,
 the learning behavior of gpCELL is governed by a set of hyperparameters that balance local modeling accuracy and global computational efficiency.
 Some of the hyperparameters of gpCELL are shared with kCELL but new ones are introduced to manage the AIC model selection process of the local GPs.
 Below are the descriptions of these hyperparameters:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\xi\in\left[0,100\right]$
\end_inset

:
 the chi-squared percentile defining the threshold for the spatial gating mechanism to determine the set of neighbor agents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $l>0$
\end_inset

:
 the lengthscale of the RBF kernel used for agent activation.
 It defines the smoothness of the spatialization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $k_{\text{steepness}}>0$
\end_inset

:
 the steepness of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\tanh$
\end_inset

 function used to normalize the social feedbacks 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}$
\end_inset

 into the instantaneous confidence 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\in\left]0,1\right]$
\end_inset

:
 The smoothing factor for the exponential moving average that updates the running confidence 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{C}_{\mathcal{A}_{i}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{\text{confidence}}\in\left[-1,1\right]$
\end_inset

:
 The threshold for agent destruction.
 Agents whose confidence falls below this value are pruned from the ensemble.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

:
 The complexity penalty weight used in the AIC calculation (
\begin_inset Formula $AIC=\alpha\left|\mathcal{D}_{i}\right|-2\ln\left(\hat{L}\right)$
\end_inset

).
 It controls the trade-off between the number of points retained in an agent's memory and its likelihood fit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $N_{ini}\in\mathbb{R}^{+}$
\end_inset

:
 The minimum number of points required in the initialization buffer to create an agent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $k_{\text{social}}>0$
\end_inset

:
 The number of closest agents used to compute the social baseline 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{base}$
\end_inset

 when an agent has no neighbor (
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{neighbors}}=1$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Online Stationary Modeling 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Online-Stationary-Modeling-gpCELL"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The previous sections have detailed the tripartite structure and learning rules of gpCELL.
 This section presents an empirical validation of the learning capabilities of this algorithm by comparing it with baseline algorithms on an online stationary modeling task.
 For this purpose,
 a high-dimensional robotic dataset is used.
 We demonstrate that despite the adaptation challenges introduced by using GPs as internal models,
 gpCELL offers a competitive predictive accuracy with kCELL and provides a seemingly reliable predictive uncertainty quantification.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Experimental Setup
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To ensure a direct comparison with the results presented in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Context-Ensemble-Local"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 the experiment is conducted on the SARCOS dataset,
 a standard benchmark for multi-dimensional robot inverse dynamics 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "vijayakumar2002statistical"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 We remind that 
\series bold
inverse dynamics modeling
\series default
 involves constructing a model that maps a dynamical system's motion to the internal forces or agents required to produce that motion (cf.
 definition 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "def:Inverse-dynamics-modeling"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 For the SARCOS dataset,
 the task involves mapping a 
\series bold
21-dimensional
\series default
 
\series bold
input space
\series default
 (7 joint positions,
 7 velocities and 7 accelerations) to the corresponding joint torques (
\series bold
7-dimensional output space
\series default
) of a seven degrees-of-freedom anthropomorphic robot arm (cf.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:robot-arm"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 for an illustration).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The dataset contains 44484 training and 4449 test points.
 A single pass over the training dataset is performed where each feature-target tuple (
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new},y_{new}$
\end_inset

) is presented to the models one at a time.
 No feature scaling is applied to the data,
 assuming that the global statistics are unknown beforehand.
 The goal behind this choice is to test the model's robustness to raw sensor inputs.
 gpCELL is compared with the same baseline algorithms as in the kCELL comparative study presented in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Context-Ensemble-Local"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For gpCELL,
 the local GP models are implemented using the GPX implementation of the 
\emph on
Surrogate Modeling Toolbox
\emph default
 library 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "saves2024smt"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 However,
 due to the computational complexity of GP updates,
 gpCELL runs significantly slower than kCELL.
 Thus,
 the grid-search hyperparameter optimization procedure used in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Context-Ensemble-Local"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 was not feasible within a reasonable time.
 Instead,
 gpCELL uses the same parameters as kCELL for the spatialization of agents (e.g.
 lengthscale 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

,
 gating threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi$
\end_inset

).
 The GP-specific parameters (RBF kernel,
 constant prior mean and complexity weight in AIC 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=0.2$
\end_inset

) are manually tuned.
 To maintain a predictable computational complexity suitable for learning from a data stream,
 two heuristics are applied in the gpCELL implementation:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Influence-Based Selection
\series default
:
 During the AIC model selection phase,
 only the less influential point of the memory is considered for substitution.
 The least influential point corresponds to the minimum diagonal entry in the inverse covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $K^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Single Agent Updates
\series default
:
 Only the closest agent to the input 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new}$
\end_inset

 (among neighbors) is updated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The predictive accuracy of the models is evaluated through adaptation speed,
 measured by the Area Under the Curve (AUC) of the prequential error (i.e.
 error computed on a feature-target pair 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{new},y_{new}$
\end_inset

 before learning on it),
 as well as the final Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) and Mean Absolute Error (MAE) on the test set.
 The final results are presented in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:gpCELL-Stationary-Results"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 Then,
 the uncertainty quantification capabilities of gpCELL and its agent population growth are analyzed.
\end_layout

