\begin_layout Paragraph
Synthesis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The results of this comparative study validate empirically the learning capabilities of the gpCELL algorithm.
 Consequently,
 the integration of another type of learning model for the design of a CELL learning algorithm is also validated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The key takeaways from this experiment can be summarized as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Nonlinear Representations
\series default
:
 gpCELL provides a more accurate representation of the underlying function (lowest final MAE).
 However,
 this comes at the cost of slower adaptation,
 higher errors on OoD inputs,
 and higher memory cost.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Uncertainty
\series default
:
 gpCELL provides a well-calibrated predictive variance (95% PICP) which quantifies the reliability of predictions.
 This variance offers a complementary measure of uncertainty that lies in the output space instead of the input space,
 as is the case for epistemic uncertainty estimation through the spatial organization of local expert agents.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Population Overgrowth
\series default
:
 Contrary to what was expected,
 the transition to nonlinear internal models did not naturally lead to a more parsimonious population,
 because gpCELL created more agents than kCELL despite the use of the same spatialization hyperparameters.
 This suggests that the current learning rules under the chosen hyperparameters lead to an overproduction of agents compared to their individual modeling capabilities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While gpCELL is more computationally and memory intensive than kCELL,
 the reliability of uncertainty quantification introduced by the local GPs makes it a promising candidate for safe control tasks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Conclusions and Limitations
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Conclusions-and-Limitations-gpCELL"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This chapter has detailed early exploratory works regarding the development and validation of gpCELL,
 a new instantiation of the CELL formalism designed to incorporate nonlinear,
 non-parametric modeling into local expert agents through GPs.
 Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Gaussian-Process-Regression"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 has given an overview of what is Gaussian Process Regression and how Gaussian Processes are used in Machine Learning.
 Then,
 Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Local-Expert-Agents-gpCELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 has provided a formal definition of the tripartite structure of local expert agents following the CELL formalism.
 It has introduced model selection mechanism based on the Akaike Information Criterion (AIC).
 AIC allows local expert agents to autonomously manage the complexity of their internal model depending on the local data manifold lying in their area of expertise.
 Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Learning-Rules-gpCELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 has presented the social learning rules.
 Finally,
 Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Online-Stationary-Modeling-gpCELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 presented the results of a comparative study conducted on the SARCOS benchmark dataset.
 The results have demonstrated that gpCELL could achieve superior or competitive performance with other baseline algorithms such as kCELL,
 while providing well-calibred predictive uncertainty estimates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The development and evaluation of gpCELL yields valuable insights:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Online GPs
\series default
:
 By distributing the computational load across an ensemble of local expert agents,
 gpCELL bypasses the 
\begin_inset Formula $O\left(n^{3}\right)$
\end_inset

 bottleneck of global GPs with a horizontally scaling architecture suitable for data streams.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Uncertainty Quantification
\series default
:
 The integration of GPs as the internal model of local expert agents provides a more rigorous estimation of predictive variance in the 
\emph on
output space
\emph default
.
 It complements the epistemic uncertainty estimation derived from the spatial organization of agents in the 
\emph on
input space
\emph default
.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Local Nonlinearity
\series default
:
 The experimental results show that gpCELL achieves the lowest final MAE among the compared baselines.
 It hints toward potential benefits of using nonlinear internal models able to adapt their complexity based on the local data manifold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Despite these strengths,
 the current version of gpCELL reveals several limitations that highlight promising directions for future research.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Population Overgrowth
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Contrary to what we intended,
 gpCELL created more agents than its linear counterpart,
 kCELL.
 This could be linked to the weakness of the kernel spatialization approach in higher-dimensional input spaces.
 Indeed,
 when the number of input dimensions grows,
 the Mahalanobis distance progressively loses its informativeness.
 This can lead to an over-fragmentation of the input space where each local expert agent is far from all others.
 Future works should investigate the 
\emph on
dimensionality reduction
\emph default
 or the use of 
\emph on
more complex data distribution estimators
\emph default
 like Kernel Density Estimation (KDE) for the spatial gating mechanism.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Memory and Computational Efficiency
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While gpCELL is more efficient than a global GP for online learning,
 it remains memory-intensive as it retained approximately 86% of encountered data in the memory of agents during the conducted experiment.
 A dedicated hyperparameter optimization study is needed to evaluate the trade-off between parsimony and accuracy.
 Furthermore,
 the current implementation relies on heuristics such as influence-based point selection and closest-agent only updates to maintain real-time feasability.
 These heuristics might have biased the experimental results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Social Mechanisms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike in kCELL,
 gpCELL only uses social feedbacks to destroy agents through updates of individual confidence based on social consensus estimation.
 This is analogous to a competition for survival among agents.
 gpCELL currently lacks the incorporation of additional social mechanisms into the agent update logic.
 More advanced rules could be considered for gpCELL to make agents interact and share knowledge more actively.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Better Baseline Comparison
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
gpCELL is an algorithm that adapts GPs for online learning through the CELL formalism.
 However,
 its efficiency has not been compared to established approximation methods such as Sparse Gaussian Processes (SGPs).
 A broader study on computational and memory efficiency,
 compared with classical GP adaptations,
 would help to situate gpCELL more clearly within the state of the art in GP learning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Speed Recommendation from an Industrial Perspective
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "chap:Industrial-use-Case"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This thesis has been rooted in a specific industrial challenge:
 the development of speed recommendation strategies to reduce road fatalities and environmental impact by advising drivers of an optimal speed profile they should follow.
 As established in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Introduction"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 the speed recommendation problem can be abstracted as a Human-Robot Collaboration (HRC) problem.
 This shift in perspective allows the speed recommendation problem to be decomposed into two subproblems:
 
\series bold
predicting the vehicle state 
\series default
when it is driven by a human while subject to external perturbations,
 and safely 
\series bold
exploiting this predictive model
\series default
 to provide speed recommendations.
 The relationship between HRC and Speed Recommendation is illustrated in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:HRC-Speed-Recommendation-Comparison"
