\begin_layout Section
Industrial Requirements for Speed Recommendation Systems
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Industrial-Requirements"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The technical evolution of speed recommendation systems,
 such as Intelligent Speed Assistance (ISA),
 has historically followed a trajectory from passive information retrieval aimed at informing the driver about the current legal speed limit 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ehrlichLAVIAFrenchISA2003,driscollLaviaEvaluationPotential2007"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 toward active and more intelligent recommendations.
 Initially,
 research and industrial efforts were primarily focused on the robust identification of legal speed limits by leveraging digital maps and accurate GPS positioning 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bahlmannMultimodalSpeedLimit2008,danielFusingNavigationVision2014,danielMultilevelDempsterShaferSpeed2012"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Such an approach is purely informative and ensures that the driver remains aware of the current legal speed limits.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As ISA becomes mandatory due to EU regulations 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "etsc2017isa,etsc2018isa,eu2019regulation,eu2021delegated"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 modern vehicles are no longer isolated agents but connected entities capable of gathering and processing large amounts of real-time data regarding traffic density,
 weather conditions,
 road surface quality,
 and more.
 This shift allows for a transition from static speed limit advisory to intelligent speed recommendation systems advising an optimal speed profile accounting for the dynamic complexity of the road context.
 As the industrial scope of speed recommendation expands,
 the deployment of such intelligent systems in a commercial vehicle requires satisfying a rigorous set of requirements which are categorized in the remainder of this section.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Multi-Objective Optimization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In contrast to purely advisory ISA systems,
 intelligent speed recommendation acts as a high-level controller that solves a multi-objective optimization problem.
 To provide tangible added value for stakeholders such as fleet managers,
 the system requires the definition of a cost function involving potentially conflicting objectives.
 Typical examples include:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Energy Efficiency
\series default
:
 Minimizing fuel consumption in internal combustion and hybrid vehicles,
 and minimizing electrical energy use in electric vehicles,
 through regenerative braking and optimal electric motor torque setpoints.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Travel-Time Efficiency
\series default
:
 Reducing trip duration while maintaining a smooth speed profile.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Comfort
\series default
:
 Avoiding abrupt acceleration or deceleration,
 as well as excessive acceleration jerk,
 which may degrade ride comfort or increase driver cognitive load.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Safety And Legal Guarantees
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Speed recommendations must be strictly bounded by physical and legal constraints.
 These act as hard state constraints:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Legal Compliance
\series default
:
 Ensuring the recommended speed profile does not exceed applicable legal speed limits,
 including conditional or temporary regulations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Safety
\series default
:
 Accounting for dynamic elements of the driving context to prevent unsafe operating conditions.
 Examples include ensuring adequate safety margins in curves,
 respecting inter-vehicle distances,
 and adapting to degraded road friction conditions to mitigate the risks of aquaplaning or loss of control.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Overridability and Driver Acceptance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As specified in the European regulation 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "eu2021delegated"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 the human remains the final decision maker over the recommendation system.
 Furthermore,
 as speed recommendation can be formulated as a HRC problem (cf.
 Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Introduction"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 driver acceptance is a major factor determining the effectiveness of the recommendation system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Overridability
\series default
:
 As the driver maintains full authority on his vehicle,
 the speed is recommended and not enforced.
 This differentiate the speed recommendation problem from the speed control problem in autonomous driving.
 The driver should be able to 
\emph on
refuse
\emph default
,
 
\emph on
delay
\emph default
 or 
\emph on
accept
\emph default
 the recommended speed at any time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Acceptance
\series default
:
 Acceptance is influenced both by how recommendations are generated and how they are communicated to the driver through a Human-Machine Interface (HMI).
 Such interface may range from purely visual indications on the vehicle dashboard to more active modalities,
 such as haptic feedback on the accelerator pedal 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "zhaoHowDoesMural2022,ehrlichLAVIAFrenchISA2003"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
 the objective is to recommend a plausible speed setpoint and to communicate it in the best way possible to the driver.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Robustness to Uncertainty and Diversity of Road Contexts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The recommendation system should maintain its efficiency across a diverse range of Operational Design Domains (ODDs) characterized by high environmental and behavioral variability:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Contextual Diversity
\series default
:
 Drivers and vehicles operate in highly different conditions (e.g.
 urban vs rural,
 varying weather conditions,
 traffic congestion).
 While many driving scenarios occur regularly,
 others are rare but safety-critical,
 requiring the system to maintain consistent performance even in low-frequency edge cases.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Heterogeneous Traffic Participants
\series default
:
 The presence of diverse road users such as cars,
 trucks,
 motorcyclists,
 cyclists,
 or even pedestrians,
 whose hard-to-predict behaviors introduces substantial stochastic variability that must be handled through robust prediction mechanisms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Certifiability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In an industrial context,
 certifiability can be defined as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Certifiability refers to the formal process required to demonstrate that a safety-critical system conforms to applicable regulatory,
 safety,
 and performance standards,
 thereby authorizing its real-world deployment 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "tambon2022certify"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset nomenclature
LatexCommand nomenclature
symbol "Certifiability"
description "Certifiability refers to the formal process required to demonstrate that a safety-critical system conforms to applicable regulatory, safety, and performance standards, thereby authorizing its real-world deployment \\cite{tambon2022certify}."
literal "true"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Advanced Driver Assistance Systems (ADAS) are subject to several requirements and standards regarding certifiability such as:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Functional Safety
\series default
 (ISO 26262 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ISO26262-1"
literal "false"

\end_inset

):
 Systems must achieve specific Automotive Safety Integrity Levels (ASIL) through rigorous Hazard Analysis and Risk Assessment (HARA),
 ensuring full traceability throughout the development lifecycle,
 from requirements to field operational tests.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Safety of the Intended Functionality
\series default
 (SOTIF - ISO/PAS 21448 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ISO21448-2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

):
 This addresses the complexity of ADAS where sensors and algorithms must interact reliably in unpredictable real-world conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Scenario-Based Validation
\series default
:
 Deployment requires comprehensive evidence gathered through simulations,
 Hardware-in-the-Loop (HIL) testing (ISO/TS 22133 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ISO22133-2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) and real-world track trials to validate performance against United Nations (UN) regulations (e.g.
 UN R171 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "UNR171"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) for driver assistance systems.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Data Integrity and Cybersecurity
\series default
 (ISO/SAE 21434 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ISO-SAE-21434-2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

):
 As modern vehicles increasingly rely on V2X (Vehicle-to-Everything) connectivity to collect road data,
 the recommendation system must remain robust against corrupted or malicious inputs that could results in unsafe speed recommendations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Deterministic Real-Time Performance
\series default
:
 Alongside functional safety,
 real-time embedded automotive systems must exhibit bounded Worst-Case Execution Times (WCET) and predictable computational load to satisfy hardware constraints of automotive Electronic Control Units (ECUs).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Proposed Architecture
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Proposed-Architecture"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The previous section exposed a comprehensive set of industrial requirements for the deployment of intelligent speed recommendation systems in real-world conditions.
 These requirements spanned from multi-objective optimization to strict certifiability constraints.
 Although substantial progress has been achieved in automated speed control for fully autonomous driving,
 speed recommendation remains a distinct challenge due to the presence of a human in the loop.
 This section presents the architecture proposed in this thesis to address the speed recommendation problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Distinguishing Recommendation and Control
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As exposed in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Introduction"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 a core distinction must be made between speed control and speed recommendation.
 In the context of autonomous driving,
 speed control aims at replacing the human driver,
 operating in a closed-loop where the controller directly manipulates vehicle actuators (e.g.
 throttle,
 brakes,
 gear box).
 In contrast,
 speed recommendation preserves full driver authority over the vehicle.
 In this context,
 the human acts as a biological actuator to the system (falling into the shared controllability challenge exposed in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:State-of-the-Art"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 The recommendation system's output is not a control signal directly applied to the vehicle's low level controls,
 but is a suggested speed setpoint that the driver may choose to 
\emph on
refuse
\emph default
,
 
\emph on
delay
\emph default
 or 
\emph on
accept
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Despite its importance for road safety and energy efficiency,
 speed recommendation has been significantly less studied than speed control.
 Research efforts have historically focused more extensively on automated control,
 often overlooking the complex modeling requirements associated with human-in-the-loop systems for effective speed recommendation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To highlight the specific research gaps of the literature regarding speed control and speed recommendation,
 we re-evaluate the body of literature introduced in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Introduction"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 by applying the technical taxonomies established in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:State-of-the-Art"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 This classification allows us to visualize the current state of the art regarding 
\series bold
control and recommendation
\series default
 strategies (in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Paper-Classification-Speed-Recommendation-Strategies"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 and 
\series bold
predictive modeling
\series default
 through learning approaches (in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Paper-Classification-Speed-Recommendation-Learning"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 It should be specified that Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Paper-Classification-Speed-Recommendation-Learning"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 does not include any online learning method like those presented in this thesis (i.e.
 continuously learning continuously a dynamics model).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As shown in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Paper-Classification-Speed-Recommendation-Strategies"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 there is a clear absence of research works exploring the combination between Online Learning approaches and MPC.
 Furthermore,
 Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Paper-Classification-Speed-Recommendation-Learning"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 reveals that the majority of research relies on 
\series bold
global parametric models
\series default
 with no existing works,
 to our knowledge,
 leveraging a 
\series bold
continuously learning,
 local non-parametric approaches
\series default
 to model the dynamics of the driving context.
\end_layout

