\begin_layout Paragraph
Multi-Objective Optimization 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The choice of the MPC framework naturally addresses the multiple,
 
\emph on
potentially conflicting objectives
\emph default
.
 By defining an appropriate cost function,
 the system can jointly optimize:
 energy efficiency,
 travel time,
 and comfort for a given driving context to produce a speed recommendation that represents the optimal tradeoff.
 With an appropriate solver,
 MPC allows the simultaneous handling of conflicting objectives within a conventional optimization framework.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Safety and Legal Constraints
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Safety-critical systems must respect hard constraints.
 Within the MPC loop,
 
\emph on
Safety
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
Legal
\emph default
 constraints requirements are handled as hard state and input constraints evaluated over the trajectory predicted by the CELL model.
 Using appropriate solvers within MPC ensures that the recommended speed profile is inherently compliant with legal speed limits and physical safety criteria (e.g.
 curvature-based speed limits or maximum inter-vehicle distances,
 ...).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Certifiability and Computational Predictability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A major challenge for the deployment of learning-based ADAS is the requirement for 
\emph on
Worst-Case Execution Times
