nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Safety through Epistemic Uncertainty Estimation and Introspection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A major risk associated with deploying a learned predictive model in safety-critical systems lies in its Out-of-Distribution (OoD) behavior.
 Some types of learned models,
 such as neural networks,
 are known to exhibit systematic overconfidence even in unobserved regions of the state space 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "guo2017calibration"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 In the context of speed recommendation,
 such overconfidence can result in unsafe or misleading predictions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As demonstrated in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Context-Ensemble-Local"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Solving-Control-Tasks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 the CELL instantiations,
 and more specifically kCELL,
 mitigate this issue by leveraging the spatial organization of local expert agents to improve the reliability of the learned predictive model within an MPC setting.
 Given that each agent is responsible for a localized region of the input space,
 the model can estimate espistemic uncertainty based on the value of activation of the agents.
 This allows the model to 
\emph on
know when it does not know.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From an industrial perspective,
 this introspective property enables the implementation of safety guardrails.
 When the recommendation system detects high epistemic uncertainty,
 it can automatically trigger the use of a fallback strategy (e.g.
 reverting to a simple legal speed limit advisory system) instead of providing a potentially erroneous or unsafe recommendation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Certifiability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The requirement for Worst-Case Execution Time (WCET) is crucial when working with a limited embedded hardware configuration.
 In Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Solving-Control-Tasks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 we demonstrated that the kCELL was fully compatible with gradient-based high-performance Quadratic Programming (QP) solvers through its local linearization capabilities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By integrating kCELL with high-performance solvers such as OSQP 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "stellato2020osqp"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 or qpOASES 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ferreau2014qpoases"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 within an MPC loop,
 we leverage solvers with established convergence properties suitable for real-time applications 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ferreau2014qpoases,stellato2020osqp"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 These solvers are designed for efficient computation in MPC loops and have been analyzed for worst-case complexity under standard assumptions (e.g.
 moderate receding horizon) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ferreau2014qpoases"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This approach would theoretically allow speed recommendations to be computed within strict computational budget,
 even with a continuously learning kCELL predictive model.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ferreau2014qpoases,stellato2020osqp"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Contextual Integrity and Parameter Isolation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In terms of certifiability,
 a conflict arises from the need to reconcile continuous online learning with the static and verifiable designs required by ISO 26262 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ISO26262-1"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Global parametric models (e.g.
 neural networks) pose a significant risk in an online learning setting:
 online updates may inadvertently cause performance drops in unrelated regions of the state space because any parameter may change.
 This phenomenon,
 known as 
\series bold
catastrophic forgetting
\series default
 (cf.
 Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:State-of-the-Art"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 undermines predictability and hinders certifiability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The CELL-based models like kCELL mitigate this issue through 
\series bold
Parameter Isolation
\series default
.
 Because learning is local,
 updates to an agent specialized in 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Highway driving
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 do not alter the behavior of agents specialized in 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Urban driving
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 because the corresponding subsets of local expert agents are spatially separable in the input space (as illustrated in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Simplified-Parameter-Isolation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 This property provides 
\series bold
Contextual Integrity
\series default
 to the continuously learning predictive model.
 It ensures that speed recommendations always rely on the model learned for the current driving context.
\end_layout

