caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 has introduced the industrial context and motivation of this research work related to the development of Intelligent Speed Recommendation.
 A distinction has been made between 
\series bold
speed control
\series default
,
 i.e automated driving,
 and 
\series bold
speed recommendation
\series default
.

\series bold
 
\series default
As this distinction emphasizes on handling the human in the loop,
 it has led to the abstraction of the speed recommendation problem into a Human-Robot Collaboration (HRC) problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This chapter argues that for such system to be effective,
 it must possess two essential capabilities:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the ability to continuously 
\series bold
learn and adapt
\series default
 to the non-stationary,
 uncertain,
 intent of the driver;
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
and the ability to 
\series bold
reliably exploit that model
\series default
 within a control framework.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This allowed to identify two primary research axes:
 
\series bold
online learning of non-stationary dynamics
\series default
 and 
\series bold
reliability in learning-based control
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Literature Review
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The literature review presented in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:State-of-the-Art"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 positions this thesis work within the state of the art.
 By analyzing the challenges of optimal control with learned models,
 we have identified 
\series bold
Model Predictive Control (MPC)
\series default
 as the most suitable framework for HRC.
 Indeed,
 MPC offers a theoretical framework to handle constraints and integrate a continuously updating predictive model.
 By analyzing the machine learning research axis,
 we have identified 
\series bold
Catastrophic Forgetting
\series default
 as a major challenge for online adaptation.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We have deemed
\series bold
 Local learning algorithms
\series default
 promising to tackle this challenge.
 These learning methods isolate parameters through spatial decomposition of the learning problem,
 offering a structural solution to the Catastrophic Forgetting problem.
 This has led to the exploration of a specific research niche:
 
\series bold
self-organizing multi-agent systems for local learning
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
The CELL Formalism and its Instantiations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To address the online learning problem,
 we have introduced the Context Ensemble Local Learning (CELL) formalism in Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Context-Ensemble-Local"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 It provides a 
\series bold
framework for designing online learning algorithms
\series default
 by treating the learning as a 
\series bold
social process
\series default
 where local expert agents self-organize to approximate a function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We have proposed a first instantiation:
 
\series bold
oCELL
\series default
.
 oCELL uses a spatialization of local expert agents based on orthotopes and has demonstrated competitive performance with state of the art learning algorithms on a simple 2D benchmark.
 The development and empirical validation of oCELL,
 has also allowed to identify emerging introspective properties.
 These properties can be derived from the result of the spatial organization of local expert agents,
 and they can be linked to the traditional notions of explainability and interpretability in machine learning.
 However,
 oCELL revealed some limitations,
 such as 
\emph on
hard-to-tune hyperparameters
\emph default
,
 the weakness of the 
\emph on
uniform knowledge assumption
\emph default
 and the 
\emph on
inefficiency of orthotopes
\emph default
 in representing complex local manifolds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In response to the limitations of oCELL,
 we have developed a new instantiation of CELL:
 
\series bold
kCELL
\series default
.
 Instead of using orthotopes for the spatialization of agents,
 kCELL uses a smooth,
 continuous approach based on kernels.
 It also introduces more sophisticated social rules to reduce the dependence on some hard-to-tune hyperparameters.
 It proved capable of 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
adapting to non-stationary environments,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
simultaneously maintaining a transparent spatialization of knowledge,
 and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
competing with state-of-the-art online learning algorithms in terms of accuracy on a inverse modeling task.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The goal behind the development of kCELL was to build upon oCELL to build a learning algorithm better suited for being integrated into MPC.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Integration into the Control Loop
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As a robust learning algorithm has been designed through kCELL,
 Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Solving-Control-Tasks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 addresses the exploitation problem.
 We have demonstrated that kCELL could be implemented efficiently for real-time applications using GPU acceleration and geospatial indexing.
 Moreover,
 we have showed that the 
\series bold
introspective properties
\series default
 of the kCELL ensemble of local expert agents can be leveraged to improve 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the learning of the predictive model within a control loop by guiding 
\series bold
exploration
\series default
 toward unknown but relevant regions;
 and 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the reliability of the resulting model by safeguarding 
\series bold
exploitation
\series default
,
 discouraging the MPC optimizer from selecting Out-of-Distribution (OoD) trajectories that lie in unknown regions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Enhancing the Robustness of Learning
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While kCELL provides a measure of epistemic uncertainty from the spatial organization of local expert agents in the 
\series bold
input space
\series default
,
 it does not provide an uncertainty measure on the predictions of local models in the 
\series bold
output space
\series default
.
 Recognizing this need for more formal uncertainty quantification for robust control,
 Chapter 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Scalable-Non-Linear-CELL"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 
\series bold
returned to the modeling layer
\series default
 to explore the 
\series bold
modularity
\series default
 of the CELL systems regarding the model class used by local expert agents.
 Thus,
 we have introduced gpCELL,
 in which local expert agents use 
\series bold
Gaussian Processes (GP) as internal models
\series default
.
 This allows the ensemble of local expert agents to behave like an 
\series bold
heterogeneous ensemble
\series default
,
 as agents can dynamically 
\series bold
adapt their complexity
\series default
 to the local data manifold.
 Through empirical validation,
 we have shown that gpCELL provides well-calibrated predictive variance intervals on its predictions while maintaining the computational advantages of the local learning approach over traditional GP in an online learning task.
 These confidence intervals can prove essential for safety-critical control using learned models.
\end_layout

