\begin_layout Subsection
Conceptual Contributions
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Conceptual-Contributions"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first set of contributions of this thesis works concerns the high-level framing of the speed recommendation problem:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Abstraction of Speed Recommendation into HRC
\series default
:
 We proposed a new perspective on speed recommendation by abstracting it into a HRC problem.
 This acknowledge the necessity of fully incorporating the human in the loop,
 making it essential to explicitly model the human behavior.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
System Architecture for HRC
\series default
:
 Following this abstraction,
 we proposed to solve the problem with a modular architecture combining Model Predictive Control (MPC) with an online learning predictive model.
 This architecture provides the flexibility to adapt to changing human behavior while regularly replanning through optimization,
 which is necessary for safe recommendations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Contributions to Online Learning
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Contributions-to-Online-Learning"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A significant portion of this thesis focused on the development of online learning algorithms based on a self-organizing ensemble of local expert agents.
 The contributions are twofold:
 
\series bold
Methodological
\series default
 and 
\series bold
Algorithmic
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Methodological Contributions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
The CELL formalism
\series default
:
 We introduced a novel formalism for designing online learning systems based on a self-organizing ensemble of local expert agents.
 The main innovation lies in treating learning as a social process that incorporates cooperation and competition and in providing learning and inference pipelines tailored for usage within control loops.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Algorithmic Contributions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
oCELL (Orthotope CELL)
\series default
:
 As a first proof-of-concept,
 oCELL demonstrated that spatialized multi-agent systems following the CELL formalism could achieve competitive performance against state-of-the-art learning algorithms on a 2D benchmark.
 It allowed us to identify introspective properties that link the spatial organization of agents to the traditional notions of explainability and interpretability in machine learning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
kCELL (Kernel CELL)
\series default
:
 We developed kCELL to provide a smoother,
 more continuous representation of the function underlying the data.
 It introduced social feedback rules where performance is assessed relative to neighbors rather than against an absolute threshold on error,
 enabling setting up a competitive survival mechanism to tackle the agent overgrowth problem.
 Experiments demonstrated kCELL's ability to adapt to non-stationary dynamics while providing the transparency needed for in-depth behavioral analysis,
 potentially unlocking valuable insights for model debugging.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
gpCELL (Gaussian Process CELL)
\series default
:
 To address the need for formal predictive uncertainty,
 we explored the modularity of CELL by using Gaussian Processes (GP) as the internal model of agents.
 gpCELL introduced a method based on distributed horizontal scaling to allow the use of GPs in online learning despite the inherent computational bottleneck induced by using a global GP.
 Through an AIC-based model selection process,
 agents are able to dynamically adapt their complexity to the local data manifold.
 A comparative experiment on an inverse modeling task reveals,
 well-calibrated predictive uncertainty intervals and competitive predictive accuracy compared to baseline online learning algorithms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Contributions to Control with Learned Models
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Contributions-to-Control-with-Learned-Models"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The final set of contributions of our research work corresponds to bridging the gap between learned models and control.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Efficient Integration and Differentiability
\series default
:
 In order to integrate kCELL into a MPC control loop,
 we proposed leveraging GPU parallelization or geospatial indexing for agent selection for real-time performance.
 Futrhermore,
 we showed that the trivial local linearization permitted by kCELL's structure makes it a natural fit for Sequential Quadratic Programming (SQP) optimizers,
 where the locality of the optimization matches the locality of the learned model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Introspective Exploration for Data Collection
\series default
:
 We proposed a novel strategy to guide exploration using the emerging properties of the agent ensemble (such as distance-based expertise or local disagreement).
 Validation on a pendulum control task showed that this introspective exploration consistently outperforms uninformed random strategies by targeting unknown but relevant regions of the state-action space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Introspective Exploitation for Reliability
\series default
:
 We introduced a method to safeguard the control process by using introspective properties such as the distance to the closest agents to regularize the MPC optimizer.
 By discouraging the optimizer from selecting OoD trajectories (i.e those lying far from the center of agents),
 we demonstrated an increase in the reliability of the controller,
 reducing prediction errors without the need for an external safety layer.
\end_layout

