\begin_layout Subsection
Perspectives in Online Learning with CELL
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Perspectives-in-Online-Learning"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Ablation and Synergy Studies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Future work should focus on quantifying the individual impact of the various social mechanisms in CELL learning algorithms such as the ones introduced with oCELL,
 kCELL and gpCELL.
 Systematic ablation studies are required to identify which combinations of rules maximize the learning efficiency and stability.
 Such systematic approach could be part of an iterative process whose objective would be to optimize the properties of the obtained CELL algorithm regarding constraints like predictive accuracy,
 sample efficiency or non-stationarity handling.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
High-Dimensional Spatialization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As the number of input dimensions grows,
 the spatialization approaches presented in this thesis suffer from the curse of dimensionality.
 We propose exploring spatialization mechanisms that are robust to increase in dimensionality,
 such as the use of robust similarity metrics like cosine similarity or local dimensionality reduction.
 By performing a local Principal Component Analysis (PCA) within each agent's area of expertise (from the parameters of their activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

),
 Mahalanobis could be computed in this reduced latent space to obtain the value of activation.
 This would allow agents to adapt their geometry more accuractely to the local manifold's actual variance,
 potentially using reconstruction errors to discriminate between neighbors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Perspectives in Control with Learned Models with CELL
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Perspective-in-Control-with-Learned"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Mathematical Guarantees through Trust Regions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To robustify the control process,
 it is essential to derive theoretical safety guarantees.
 As a first step,
 we propose defining a trust region around the current state 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 where the local linear approximation of the ensemble of local expert agents remains valid within a bounded error 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

.
 By solving for the maximum radius 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left\Vert \hat{f}\left(x\right)-\sum w_{i}\hat{f_{i}}\left(x\right)\right\Vert \leq\epsilon\,\,\forall x\in\mathcal{B}\left(x,r\right)
\]

\end_inset

we can provide the SQP optimizer with a dynamic trust-region constraint,
 ensuring that the trajectory search never relies on inaccurate local linearizations.
 Because kCELL uses linear models,
 a closed-form analytical solution for this problem could probably be derived under the right assumptions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Graph-Based Optimization Initialization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Gradient-based optimizers such as SQP are highly sensitive to initial conditions 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "boggs1995sequential"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 A promising perspective is to treat the ensemble of agents as a connected graph.
 An agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{0}$
\end_inset

 would be connected to an agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{1}$
\end_inset

 if the area of expertise of agent 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{1}$
\end_inset

 intersects with the set of possible predictive outputs of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 By searching for a promising chain of successive agents that coul potentially connect the current state to the goal,
 a warm-start trajectory could be generated to initialize the optimizer.
 This high-level topological planning would guide the low-level numerical optimization away from local minima and provide a feasible (or close-to-feasible) solution if hard constraints are considered.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
LQR-based Explainer for Transparency of Decision Making
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While CELL instantiations like kCELL can provide full transparency in the prediction process,
 the optimization process often remains a black-box opacifying the decision making pipeline,
 specifically under hard constraints.
 We propose a post-hoc explanatory explanation method based on the Linear Quadratic Regulator (LQR) 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kalman1964linear"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Following an optimization with SQP (with linearization of kCELL),
 the kCELL model can be linearized around the obtained trajectory to compute an unconstrained LQR baseline control law using the Riccati equations.
 The resulting local linear control law 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{t}=-G_{t}s_{t}
\]

\end_inset

provides a transparent linear relationship between the states and actions.
 By comparing the constrained SQP cost to the LQR cost
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta J=J_{SQP}-J_{LQR}
\]

\end_inset

we can quantify the exact 
\series bold
cost of constrains
\series default
.
 Furthermore,
 the 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{t}$
\end_inset

 matrix can be used as a feature importance metric,
 bringing another justification layer to the decision making process.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Robust Probabilistic MPC
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 the predictive variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{*}^{2}$
\end_inset

 provided by gpCELL enables the transition from deterministic to probabilistic constraint handling.
 Indeed,
 hard constraints 
\begin_inset Formula $g\left(s_{t},u_{t}\right)\leq0$
\end_inset

 can be transformed into chance constraints such as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P\left(g\left(s_{t},u_{t}\right)\leq0\right)\geq1-\alpha
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in\left[0,1\right]$
\end_inset

 allows to set the minimal probability at which the constraint must be satisfied.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using a first-order Taylor expansion and assuming a Gaussian distribution of the predicted state 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}\left(\mu_{t},\Sigma_{t}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 this can be reformulated as a deterministic constraint:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
g\left(\mu_{t},u_{t}\right)+\Phi^{-1}\left(1-\alpha\right)\sqrt{\nabla g^{\top}\Sigma_{t}\nabla g}\leq0
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{-1}$
\end_inset

 is the inverse cumulative distribution function.
 This approach would allow the controller to maintain safety margins that adapt dynamically to the model's confidence.
 In the context of HRC,
 it would provide a more reliable synergy with the driver in uncertain environments where safety is critical.
\end_layout

